var ZAIKA_EXTRA_EN={"biryani":{"intro":"Biryani is the dish most homes save for guests and celebrations, and Karachi style is known for bold spice, separate grains of rice, and the smell of dum that fills the house before the lid comes off. The method below keeps the chicken juicy and the rice fragrant without turning the layers into one soft mass. Marination does the heavy flavour work, so the cooking itself is mostly layering and waiting. Serve it straight from the pot, with raita and salad, and let people take second helpings while it is still steaming.","ing":["Chicken 750g","yoghurt 1 cup","Basmati rice 3 cups (soaked)","Biryani masala 3 tbsp","ginger-garlic paste 2 tbsp","Onions 3 (browned)","Tomatoes 2","green chillies 4","Fresh coriander","mint","lemon","Saffron / food colour","ghee 3 tbsp","Salt","bay leaves","cardamom","cloves (for the rice water)"],"steps":["Wash the rice until the water runs clear, soak it for 30 minutes, and drain. Mix the yoghurt, biryani masala, ginger-garlic paste and salt into the chicken and marinate for 30 minutes.","Boil a large pot of water with salt and the whole spices. Add the rice and cook until it is 70 percent done and still firm in the centre, then drain at once.","Heat the ghee, brown the onions until deep golden, and remove half for layering. Keep the rest in the pan.","Add the tomatoes and the marinated chicken, and fry on medium high heat for 10 to 12 minutes, until the masala thickens and the oil separates.","Spread the chicken masala in a heavy pot. Add half the rice, then sprinkle coriander, mint, brown onion, lemon and saffron.","Add the remaining rice and top with the rest of the onion, herbs and saffron and a spoon of ghee. Seal the lid with a cloth or foil.","Cook on low heat on dum for 20 minutes without opening the lid, then rest for 10 minutes off the heat.","Open the lid, fluff gently from the sides with a fork, mix lightly so the colours stay visible, and serve hot."],"tip":"Do not over-boil the rice. It will finish cooking on dum. Put a cloth over the lid on dum so the steam does not escape, and taste the rice water. It should taste slightly salty, because plain rice needs that seasoning.","notes":["Chicken on the bone stays juicier during dum. Use leg pieces and trim away excess fat.","Aged basmati gives separate grains. New rice turns soft more easily, so watch the boiling time.","Full fat yoghurt makes the marinade cling to the chicken. Low fat yoghurt can split when it hits heat.","Biryani masala varies by brand. Taste the masala before adding extra chilli, and adjust next time, not now."],"variations":["Vegetable biryani: replace the chicken with potatoes, cauliflower, peas and carrots, marinate the vegetables briefly, and shorten the dum to 15 minutes.","Mutton biryani: use 750g mutton, cook the masala until the meat is nearly tender before layering, and allow 30 minutes on dum.","Less spicy: halve the green chillies, skip extra red chilli, and serve with extra raita."],"serving":"Serves 4 generously with raita, kachumber salad and lemon wedges. Put the pot in the centre of the table and open it there, so everyone gets the dum aroma. Naan is optional, because the rice is the main dish. A glass of lassi or a mint drink balances the spice well.","storage":"Cool within 1 hour and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 2 days. Reheat in a covered pan on low heat with 2 tbsp of water, or in the microwave covered, until steaming hot throughout. Do not reheat more than once. Freeze in portions for up to 1 month, and thaw in the fridge overnight before reheating.","mistakes":["Boiling the rice fully before dum. It turns mushy in the pot.","Opening the lid during dum. Steam escapes and the rice stays undercooked.","Using too little salt in the rice water. The layers taste flat.","Stirring hard after dum. Grains break and the colours mix into one shade."],"faq":[["Can I make biryani without saffron?","Yes. Use a pinch of yellow food colour dissolved in warm milk, or skip it. The flavour comes from the masala and the dum, not from colour alone."],["How long should the chicken marinate?","30 minutes is the minimum. Two to four hours in the fridge is better. Keep it refrigerated, and do not leave marinated chicken at room temperature."],["Why is my rice sticky?","The rice was overboiled, or it was not drained at once, or the pot was stirred hard. Drain at 70 percent done and rest the pot before fluffing."],["Can I use boneless chicken?","Yes. Use thigh pieces and reduce the masala cooking by 3 to 4 minutes, so the chicken does not dry out."]]},"nihari":{"intro":"Nihari is slow food in the truest sense, a weekend stew that rewards patience with meat so tender it leaves the bone and a gravy that coats the spoon. In this version beef shank cooks gently for about two hours until the connective tissue softens and the masala deepens in colour and flavour. The flour slurry at the end is what gives nihari its famous thick, glossy finish, so it must go in slowly and be stirred well. Serve it bubbling hot with fresh naan and the classic garnish, and keep extra lemon on the table.","ing":["Beef (shank) 1 kg","Nihari masala 4 tbsp","flour 3 tbsp","Ginger-garlic paste 2 tbsp","onion 1","Ghee / oil 1/2 cup","Ginger","green chilli","lemon (for garnish)","Fresh coriander"],"steps":["Heat the ghee, lightly brown the sliced onion without burning it, add the ginger-garlic paste, and fry for 1 minute until the raw smell goes.","Add the beef and the nihari masala and fry on medium high heat for 8 to 10 minutes, until the meat changes colour and the masala coats every piece.","Add enough hot water to cover the meat by about 2 inches, bring it to a boil, and skim off any foam.","Cover the pot and cook on low heat for 2 hours. Stir now and then, and add hot water if the level drops below the meat.","Check the meat. It should shred with a fork and leave the bone easily. If it is still firm, cook for 20 to 30 minutes more.","Mix the flour with 1 cup of cool water until completely smooth, with no lumps. Pour it in a thin stream while stirring the pot.","Simmer for 10 to 15 minutes, stirring often, until the gravy is thick and glossy and coats the back of a spoon.","Rest for 10 minutes, taste for salt, and serve with naan, garnished with ginger, green chilli, lemon and coriander."],"tip":"Do not keep the gravy thin. Nihari is known for its thick, glossy gravy. Add the flour slowly so no lumps form, and stir constantly while it goes in.","notes":["Shank, with bone and connective tissue, is ideal for nihari. It gives body to the gravy. Boneless stewing beef works, but the gravy is less rich.","Nihari masala varies in strength by brand. Use the stated amount the first time, and adjust next time.","Ghee gives the traditional flavour. Oil works too, and half ghee and half oil is a good middle way.","Flour is there for thickness only. Do not add extra, because it dulls the spice."],"variations":["Mutton nihari: use the same method and cook for about 1 hour and 30 minutes, checking earlier, because mutton softens sooner.","Chicken nihari: use bone-in chicken, cook for 35 to 40 minutes, add the flour slurry sooner, and expect a lighter gravy.","Spicier: add extra green chillies at serving, not during cooking, so the heat stays fresh."],"serving":"Serves 5 with naan and small bowls of garnish on the table, so each person can add ginger, chilli, lemon and coriander to taste. Nihari is traditionally a breakfast or weekend lunch dish, served in deep bowls with plenty of gravy spooned over the meat. Keep the naan warm and bring the pot to the table while it is still bubbling.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 3 days. The gravy thickens as it cools. Reheat gently on low heat with a splash of hot water, stirring until steaming hot throughout. Freeze for up to 2 months, thaw overnight in the fridge, and add the garnish fresh at serving only.","mistakes":["Cooking on high heat. The meat toughens and the gravy reduces too fast.","Adding the flour dry or all at once. Lumps form and cannot be stirred out easily.","Skimping on cooking time. The meat stays chewy instead of falling off the bone.","Serving with cold naan and no garnish. The flavour feels flat without them."],"faq":[["Why is my nihari gravy thin?","The flour was too little, or the simmer after adding it was too short. Simmer for 10 to 15 minutes while stirring. If needed, add 1 extra tbsp of flour dissolved in water."],["Can I use a pressure cooker?","Yes. Cook the meat and masala for 25 to 30 minutes after pressure is reached, then finish in an open pot with the flour slurry. Follow your cooker instructions for safety."],["Is nihari very spicy?","The masala gives warmth, not burning heat. Fresh chillies at serving let each person choose how hot their bowl is."],["Can I make it a day ahead?","Yes. The flavour improves overnight in the fridge. Reheat gently, add a little hot water if it is too thick, and add the garnish fresh."]]},"karahi":{"intro":"Chicken karahi is dhaba food at its best, cooked fast in a deep wok, with no onion and no long list of spices to hide behind. Fresh tomatoes break down into the masala, ginger and green chillies go in at the end, and the whole dish is ready in about 40 minutes. The trick is heat and timing. Keep the flame high, let the tomatoes do their work, and finish with ginger while the karahi is still sizzling. Eat it straight from the kadhai with hot roti or naan, while the masala is glossy and the chicken is tender.","ing":["Chicken 750g","tomatoes 4 large","Ginger-garlic paste 1 tbsp","Green chillies 4","ginger julienne","Ghee / oil 1/2 cup","Salt","crushed red chilli","garam masala","Fresh coriander"],"steps":["Cut the tomatoes into quarters and keep the ginger, chillies and coriander ready. Karahi moves fast, so everything must be within reach.","Heat the ghee in a kadhai on high heat. Add the chicken and fry for 4 to 5 minutes, turning often, until the outside changes colour.","Add the ginger-garlic paste and fry for 1 minute. Add the tomatoes and a little salt, cover, and cook for 8 to 10 minutes on medium high heat.","Uncover and mash the softened tomatoes with the back of a spoon. Keep frying until the masala thickens and the oil rises to the top.","Add the crushed red chilli and taste for salt. If the masala looks dry, add 2 tbsp of hot water only, no more.","Add half the ginger julienne and two sliced green chillies. Fry for 2 more minutes so their flavour goes into the masala.","Sprinkle garam masala, cover, and give 5 minutes of dum on low heat. The chicken should be cooked through and still juicy.","Top with the remaining ginger, chillies and fresh coriander. Serve at once, straight from the kadhai, with roti or naan."],"tip":"Do not add water to karahi. The tomatoes own water is enough. Keep the heat high so the chicken stays tender and the masala is well fried, and add the fresh ginger at the end, not at the start.","notes":["Use ripe, soft tomatoes. Hard tomatoes take longer to break down and leave the masala watery.","Bone-in chicken gives more flavour and stays juicy. Boneless thigh also works, but cook it for 3 to 4 minutes less.","Ghee gives the dhaba flavour. Oil works, but use a little extra ginger at the end to lift it.","Fresh ginger julienne is a garnish and a flavour. Dried ginger powder is not a substitute here."],"variations":["Mutton karahi: use 750g mutton, cook covered for 40 to 50 minutes until tender, then fry the masala on high heat as usual.","Green chilli karahi: skip red chilli, use 8 green chillies, half in the masala and half as garnish, for a fresh, sharp heat.","Butter karahi: finish with 1 tbsp of butter and 2 tbsp of cream, stirred in off the heat, for a richer, milder version."],"serving":"Serves 4 with roti or naan, a simple salad of onion and lemon, and raita if you like it. Serve in the kadhai itself, with lemon wedges on the side, and eat while it is hot. The masala is meant to be scooped up with bread, so keep extra roti ready. A cold drink or lassi helps with the heat.","storage":"Cool within 1 hour and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 2 days. Reheat in a kadhai on medium heat, covered, with 1 to 2 tbsp of water, until steaming hot. Add fresh ginger and coriander after reheating. Freezing is possible for up to 1 month, but the tomato masala softens on thawing, so fresh is best.","mistakes":["Adding water at the start. The masala turns into a thin curry instead of a thick, glossy karahi.","Cooking on low heat throughout. The chicken steams, the tomatoes stay chunky, and the masala never fries properly.","Adding ginger at the start only. It cooks away, and the fresh finish is lost.","Overcooking boneless chicken. It dries out quickly at high heat, so check it early."],"faq":[["Why does my karahi have no masala?","The tomatoes were not mashed and fried long enough, or water was added. Mash the tomatoes well and fry until the oil separates."],["Can I make karahi without a kadhai?","Yes. Use a deep, heavy wok or frying pan. A thin pan burns the masala, so keep the heat a little lower and stir more."],["Is karahi supposed to have onion?","Traditional Pakistani karahi has no onion. The tomato water makes the masala. Onion makes a different dish, closer to a curry."],["How do I know the chicken is cooked?","The juices run clear when a piece is cut at the thickest part, and the meat is no longer pink. If unsure, cut the largest piece to check."]]},"daal":{"intro":"Daal fry is the everyday dish that proves simple food can still be memorable, provided the daal is cooked soft and the tadka is poured on hot and covered at once. Masoor and moong cook quickly and turn creamy without any cream, and the ghee, garlic, cumin and whole red chillies on top give the aroma that reaches the table before the bowl does. This is comfort food for rice or roti, for busy weeknights and for days when nothing fancy is needed. The whole recipe takes about 35 minutes, and most of that is the daal quietly boiling.","ing":["Masoor / moong daal 1½ cups","Turmeric 1/2 tsp","salt","Garlic 4 cloves","cumin 1 tsp","Whole red chillies 3","ghee 3 tbsp","Green chilli","fresh coriander","lemon"],"steps":["Rinse the daal in several changes of water until the water is mostly clear, then drain it well.","Put the daal in a pot with turmeric and 4 cups of water. Bring it to a boil, skim off the foam, and reduce the heat.","Simmer for 20 to 25 minutes, stirring now and then, until the daal is completely soft and creamy. Add hot water if it gets too thick.","Taste and add salt. The daal should be pourable, not stiff, because it thickens as it cools.","Slice the garlic thinly. Heat the ghee in a small pan on medium heat and add the cumin. When it sizzles, add the garlic.","Fry until the garlic is golden, not brown. Add the whole red chillies, count to ten, and take the pan off the heat at once.","Pour the hot tadka over the daal in one go, listen for the sizzle, and cover the pot for 2 minutes so the aroma stays in.","Stir in the green chilli and coriander, squeeze lemon over the top, and serve hot with rice or roti."],"tip":"Cover the daal as soon as you pour the tadka. This keeps the aroma inside the daal instead of letting it escape into the kitchen, and it lets the garlic flavour spread through the whole pot.","notes":["Masoor daal cooks fastest and turns creamy. Moong daal holds a little more shape. Either works, and a half and half mix is also good.","Turmeric is for colour and gentle flavour. Too much makes the daal bitter, so measure it.","Slice the garlic, do not crush it for the tadka. Slices turn golden evenly and stay crisp.","Ghee carries the tadka flavour best. Oil works, but add an extra clove of garlic to make up for it."],"variations":["Palak daal: stir 2 cups of chopped spinach into the cooked daal and simmer for 5 minutes before the tadka.","Tomato daal: add 1 chopped tomato with the daal at the start, for a tangy, slightly thicker result.","Extra garlicky: double the garlic in the tadka and add half a tsp of crushed red chilli with it."],"serving":"Serves 4 with steamed rice or roti, a wedge of lemon, and a simple salad or pickle on the side. Daal is best served loose and hot, in deep bowls, with the tadka visible on top. If serving with rice, spoon the daal over the rice at the table. A fried egg or a piece of papad turns it into a fuller meal.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 3 days. The daal thickens in the fridge, so stir in a splash of hot water when reheating. Reheat on low heat, stirring, until steaming hot, and add fresh coriander after reheating. Freeze for up to 1 month in portions, thaw in the fridge, and make a fresh tadka for the best aroma.","mistakes":["Undercooking the daal. Grainy daal never turns creamy, however good the tadka is.","Burning the garlic. Brown garlic turns bitter and spoils the whole pot, so take the pan off the heat early.","Leaving the pot uncovered after the tadka. The aroma escapes and the daal tastes flat.","Adding salt at the very start in hard water areas can slow softening, so season after the daal is soft if your daal stays firm."],"faq":[["Which daal is best for tadka daal?","Masoor is quickest and creamiest. Moong is a close second. Toor daal also works, but it needs longer cooking and a pressure cooker helps."],["Can I make daal fry without ghee?","Yes. Use oil and add an extra clove of garlic. The result is lighter, and the tadka still works, but the aroma is less rich."],["Why is my daal watery?","It was not simmered long enough, or too much water was used. Simmer uncovered for a few minutes to thicken it, and remember it thickens more as it cools."],["Can I cook the daal in a pressure cooker?","Yes. Cook for 2 whistles or about 8 minutes at pressure, let the pressure release naturally, then finish with the tadka in an open pot."]]},"chapli":{"intro":"Chapli kebab is Peshawar on a plate, flat, soft, richly spiced, and fried on a tawa until the edges turn crisp. The name comes from its flat shape, and the flavour comes from tomato, pomegranate seeds, crushed whole coriander and green chillies mixed right into the mince. It is street food and celebration food at once, eaten with naan, onion and green chutney, and best made with cold mince and gentle hands. Fry it on medium low heat so the centre cooks through without burning the outside, and press a piece of tomato into the middle, as the kebab makers do.","ing":["Beef mince 500g","Tomato 1 finely chopped","onion 1","Pomegranate seeds 1 tbsp","crushed whole coriander","Green chillies 3","ginger","salt","Maize flour 2 tbsp","Oil for frying"],"steps":["Chop the onion and tomato very finely and squeeze out excess tomato juice, so the mix stays firm enough to shape.","Put the mince in a bowl with the tomato, onion, pomegranate seeds, crushed coriander, chillies, ginger, salt and maize flour. Mix with your hands until just combined.","Cover and rest the mix in the fridge for 20 minutes. Cold mince holds its shape and fries better.","Wet your hands with cold water. Take a portion, flatten it into a thin round about 4 inches across, and press a small piece of tomato into the centre.","Heat a generous layer of oil on a tawa on medium heat. The oil should be hot enough to sizzle, but not smoking.","Slide the kebabs in, leaving space between them. Fry for 4 to 5 minutes on the first side without pressing them down.","Turn gently with a wide spatula and fry for 4 to 5 minutes more, until golden on both sides and cooked through.","Lift out, drain briefly, and serve hot with green chutney, sliced onion and naan."],"tip":"Do not press the kebabs too much. Turn them gently or they will break. Cold mince makes better kebabs, so keep the mix in the fridge until you shape each batch.","notes":["Use mince with some fat, about 15 to 20 percent. Very lean mince makes dry, crumbly kebabs.","Maize flour binds the mix and gives the classic texture. Wheat flour works, but the kebab is denser.","Pomegranate seeds give a sour pop. Crush them lightly, do not grind them to a paste.","Finely chopped tomato adds moisture and flavour, but too much juice makes the kebab fall apart, so drain it first."],"variations":["Chicken chapli: use chicken mince, add 1 extra tbsp of maize flour, and fry for 3 to 4 minutes per side, because chicken cooks faster.","Extra spicy: add 1 tsp of crushed red chilli and an extra green chilli, and serve with extra chutney.","Chapli burger: put a kebab in a toasted bun with onion, tomato, chutney and a fried egg, for a Peshawar style burger."],"serving":"Serves 4 as a main dish with naan, green chutney, sliced onion, lemon and a salad. Serve 2 kebabs per person with plenty of chutney, and keep extra naan warm. Chapli kebab also works as a starter, one kebab per person with chutney and lemon, before a main course. Mint raita cools the spice nicely.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate cooked kebabs in an airtight container for up to 2 days. Reheat on a tawa on medium heat until hot through, or in the oven at 180C for 8 to 10 minutes. Uncooked mix keeps in the fridge for up to 1 day. Freeze shaped, uncooked kebabs between sheets of baking paper for up to 1 month, and fry from frozen, adding 2 minutes per side.","mistakes":["Shaping thick kebabs. The outside burns before the centre cooks, so keep them thin and even.","Frying on very high heat. The spices burn and the kebab breaks when turned.","Pressing with the spatula. It squeezes out the juices and makes the kebab dry.","Using warm mince straight from the shop. It is soft, sticky and hard to shape, so chill it first."],"faq":[["Why do my chapli kebabs break?","The mix was too wet, the kebab was too thick, or it was turned too soon. Drain the tomato, chill the mix, keep kebabs thin, and let the first side set before turning."],["Can I bake chapli kebabs?","Yes. Brush with oil and bake at 200C for 15 to 18 minutes, turning once. They are less crisp than fried kebabs, but still tasty."],["What is pomegranate seed for?","Anar dana gives a sour, fruity note that balances the rich mince. If you cannot find it, use 1 tsp of lemon juice and a few crushed dried cranberries, but the flavour is different."],["Can I make the mix ahead?","Yes. Mix and refrigerate for up to 1 day, covered. Shape just before frying, so the kebabs hold their form."]]},"tikka":{"intro":"Chicken tikka is the dish that teaches patience in a good way. The marinade does the work, yoghurt and spices working into the meat for at least an hour, and the grill only needs high heat and a few minutes per side. Done right, the outside is lightly charred and smoky, the inside stays juicy, and lemon and chaat masala at the end wake everything up. It works as a starter, as a main dish with naan, and sliced into wraps and salads the next day. The recipe below uses an oven grill or a barbecue, and a hot pan works too if that is what you have.","ing":["Chicken (leg / boneless) 750g","Yoghurt 1/2 cup","red chilli 1 tbsp","Turmeric","garam masala","cumin powder","Ginger-garlic paste 1 tbsp","lemon 1","Oil / butter for brushing"],"steps":["Cut the chicken into even pieces and score each piece with 2 shallow cuts, so the marinade reaches inside.","Mix the yoghurt, red chilli, turmeric, garam masala, cumin powder, ginger-garlic paste, lemon juice and salt into a smooth paste.","Coat the chicken well, cover, and refrigerate for at least 1 hour. Overnight is fine and gives a deeper flavour.","Take the chicken out 20 minutes before cooking, so it is not fridge cold when it hits the heat.","Heat the grill or barbecue to high. Oil the rack lightly and lay the chicken in a single layer, leaving space between pieces.","Grill for 6 to 8 minutes on the first side, brushing with butter or oil once, until the edges char lightly.","Turn and grill for 6 to 8 minutes more, until cooked through and the juices run clear.","Rest for 5 minutes, sprinkle lemon juice and chaat masala, and serve hot with onion, chutney and naan."],"tip":"The longer the marination, the softer the tikka. If time is short, give it at least 30 minutes, and never skip the scoring, because that is how the marinade gets into thick pieces.","notes":["Thigh meat stays juicier than breast on high heat. Breast works, but check it a minute earlier.","Hang the yoghurt or use thick yoghurt. Watery yoghurt slides off the chicken and burns on the grill.","Fresh lemon in the marinade helps tenderise, but too much lemon for too long can make the surface mushy, so keep overnight marinades lemon light.","Chaat masala goes on at the end, not in the marinade, so its tang stays bright."],"variations":["Malai tikka: replace red chilli with white pepper, add 2 tbsp of cream and 1 tbsp of cheese to the marinade, for a mild, creamy version.","Hariyali tikka: blend coriander, mint, green chillies and yoghurt into a green marinade, and grill the same way.","Tikka wraps: slice leftover tikka, roll in paratha or roti with onion, chutney and salad, for lunch the next day."],"serving":"Serves 4 with naan or roti, sliced onion, lemon wedges, green chutney and a salad. Serve on a warm platter, straight from the grill, with the lemon squeezed over at the table. Tikka also sits well on rice, in a wrap, or on top of a salad. Keep extra chutney on the side, because people always ask for more.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 2 days. Reheat in a hot oven at 180C for 8 minutes, or in a pan on medium heat, until steaming hot. Do not microwave for long, because it dries the chicken and softens the char. Marinated, uncooked chicken keeps in the fridge for up to 1 day. Freeze cooked tikka for up to 1 month and thaw in the fridge.","mistakes":["Grilling fridge cold chicken. The outside chars before the inside cooks, so bring it to room temperature first.","Crowding the grill. Pieces steam instead of charring, so leave space and cook in batches if needed.","Skipping the rest. Cutting at once lets the juices run out and the tikka dries.","Using a thin marinade. It drips off and burns, leaving the chicken bland."],"faq":[["Can I cook tikka in a pan?","Yes. Use a heavy pan on high heat with a little oil, 6 to 7 minutes per side. It will not have the same smoky char, but the flavour is close."],["How do I get the smoky flavour at home?","After grilling, place a small steel bowl with a hot charcoal piece in the pot, drizzle a few drops of oil on it, cover for 2 minutes, and remove the bowl. Keep the kitchen ventilated."],["Can I use breast chicken?","Yes, but do not overcook it. Check after 5 to 6 minutes per side, and rest it well. Thigh is more forgiving."],["Is tikka spicy?","It is warm and spiced, not fiercely hot. Reduce the red chilli to 1 tsp for a mild version, and let the chutney add heat at the table."]]},"halwapuri":{"intro":"Halwa puri is Sunday breakfast in Lahore, and in many homes it is a whole event, not just a meal. Three things arrive together, puffed puri, soft semolina halwa, and spiced chickpeas with potato, and the pleasure is in taking a little of each in one bite. Each part is simple on its own, but the timing matters. Fry the puri last, so it arrives puffed and hot, keep the halwa warm and soft, and let the chickpeas simmer until the masala coats them. Cook it on a slow morning, eat it together, and do not rush it.","ing":["Flour 2 cups","semolina 1 cup","Sugar 1 cup","ghee 1/2 cup","Chickpeas (boiled) 2 cups","Potato 1","onion","tomato","Garam masala","turmeric","red chilli","Cardamom","almonds for garnish"],"steps":["Knead the flour with water and a pinch of salt into a firm, smooth dough. Cover and rest for 20 minutes.","Boil the chickpeas if not already boiled, until soft but not mushy, and keep 1 cup of their cooking water.","Roast the semolina in ghee on low heat, stirring constantly, until it turns golden and smells nutty, about 8 to 10 minutes.","Add hot water and sugar carefully, it will bubble, stir until the halwa thickens, then add cardamom and cover on low heat for 5 minutes.","Heat oil, fry the onion until soft, add tomato, potato, garam masala, turmeric and red chilli, and fry the masala well.","Add the chickpeas and their cooking water, simmer for 10 minutes, and mash a few chickpeas against the side to thicken the gravy.","Roll small puris, no thicker than a coin edge, and deep fry in hot oil, pressing gently so each one puffs. Drain on paper.","Serve puri, halwa and chickpeas together on one plate, with the halwa garnished with almonds."],"tip":"Do not use too much flour while rolling puri, or it will not puff. If the oil is well heated, the puri puffs at once, so test with a small piece of dough first, it should rise in seconds.","notes":["Firm dough makes puffed puri. Soft dough absorbs oil and stays flat, so knead it firm and rest it well.","Fine semolina gives a smooth halwa. Coarse semolina needs more water and longer cooking.","Keep the chickpea cooking water. It thickens the gravy naturally and carries flavour.","Cardamom goes into the halwa at the end, so its aroma stays fresh instead of cooking away."],"variations":["Aloo ki tarkari instead of chickpeas: cook potatoes in the same masala, mash half of them, and serve as the savoury side.","Less sweet halwa: reduce the sugar to 3/4 cup and add a little extra cardamom, for a halwa that suits the spicy chickpeas better.","Whole wheat puri: use half whole wheat flour and half plain flour, rest the dough a little longer, and roll slightly thicker."],"serving":"Serves 4, with 2 to 3 puris per person, a spoon of halwa, and a generous ladle of chickpeas on one plate. Serve with pickle, sliced onion and lemon, and chai on the side. Halwa puri is a sit down breakfast, eaten hot, with everyone at the table together. Make extra puri, because it disappears fastest.","storage":"Eat puri fresh, it does not keep well. Halwa keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days in an airtight container, and reheats in a pan on low heat with a splash of milk or water. Chickpeas keep for up to 3 days in the fridge, and reheat on the stove until bubbling. Do not store assembled plates, keep the three parts separate and combine at serving.","mistakes":["Rolling puri too thick or with too much dry flour. It stays flat and chewy instead of puffing.","Roasting semolina on high heat. It browns in patches and tastes burnt, so keep the heat low and stir.","Adding cold water to hot semolina. It clumps, so use hot water and stir at once.","Frying puri in oil that is not hot enough. It soaks up oil and turns greasy."],"faq":[["Why does my puri not puff?","The dough was too soft, the puri was rolled too thick, or the oil was not hot enough. Knead firm, roll thin, and test the oil first."],["Can I use tinned chickpeas?","Yes. Drain and rinse them, then simmer in the masala for 10 minutes. Add a little hot water, because tinned chickpeas have no cooking water."],["How sweet should the halwa be?","Traditional halwa puri halwa is quite sweet, to balance the spicy chickpeas. If you prefer less sweet, reduce the sugar, but do not cut it by half, or the texture changes."],["Can I make parts ahead?","Yes. Make the halwa and chickpeas the day before and refrigerate. Fry the puri fresh on the morning, because reheated puri is never as good."]]},"samosa":{"intro":"A good samosa is judged in the first bite, crisp pastry that shatters, then spiced potato with peas, ginger and coriander. The pastry must be firm and rested, the filling must be dry, not wet, and the frying must be slow, on low heat, so the crust turns golden all the way through instead of browning in a minute and staying pale inside. This recipe makes the classic Punjabi samosa, the one served with green and tamarind chutney and a cup of tea. Make a double batch, because they vanish fast, and freeze the extras before frying for another day.","ing":["Flour 2 cups","carom seeds 1/2 tsp","Potatoes (boiled) 4","peas 1/2 cup","Ginger","green chilli","garam masala","Fresh coriander","pomegranate seeds","Oil for frying"],"steps":["Rub the carom seeds and 3 tbsp of oil into the flour with your fingertips until it looks like breadcrumbs.","Add water a little at a time and knead a firm, stiff dough. It should not be soft. Cover and rest for 20 minutes.","Boil the potatoes until just tender, cool them, and crumble them roughly. Do not mash them smooth.","Heat 1 tbsp of oil, fry the ginger and green chilli for 30 seconds, add the peas, garam masala, salt and pomegranate seeds, and cook for 2 minutes.","Add the potatoes and coriander, mix well, and cook for 2 to 3 minutes until the filling is dry. Cool it completely.","Divide the dough into balls, roll each into an oval, cut in half, and form a cone, sealing the straight edge with water.","Fill each cone three quarters full, seal the top edge firmly, and press to close. Check that no filling escapes.","Fry in oil on low heat for 12 to 15 minutes, turning now and then, until deep golden and crisp. Drain and serve with chutneys."],"tip":"Always fry samosas on low heat. On high heat the pastry will burn and the inside will stay raw, and the crust will bubble instead of turning crisp and even.","notes":["Firm dough is the secret to crisp pastry. Soft dough makes a chewy, oily samosa.","Cool the filling completely before filling. Warm filling steams the pastry from inside and weakens the seal.","Carom seeds give the classic flavour and help digestion. Do not skip them if you can find them.","Dry filling matters. Wet filling leaks, softens the pastry, and makes frying messy."],"variations":["Keema samosa: replace the potato with cooked, spiced mince, cooled completely, and fry the same way.","Paneer samosa: use crumbled paneer with peas, garam masala and coriander, for a richer vegetarian filling.","Baked samosa: brush with oil and bake at 190C for 20 to 25 minutes, turning once. Less crisp, but lighter."],"serving":"Serves 6, with 2 samosas per person, green chutney, tamarind chutney, sliced onion and hot chai. Serve on a platter, cut one open to show the filling, and keep extra chutney on the side. Samosas are best hot, within 30 minutes of frying, while the pastry is still crisp. A little chaat masala sprinkled on top adds a tangy finish.","storage":"Eat fresh for the best crispness. Cooled, fried samosas keep in the fridge for up to 2 days and reheat in the oven at 180C for 8 to 10 minutes, until crisp again. Do not microwave, because the pastry turns soft. Freeze uncooked, shaped samosas on a tray, then bag them for up to 1 month, and fry from frozen on low heat, adding 3 to 4 minutes.","mistakes":["Kneading a soft dough. It rolls easily, but fries soft and oily.","Filling with warm, wet filling. The seal opens and the filling leaks into the oil.","Frying on high heat. The outside browns fast and the pastry inside stays doughy.","Overfilling the cones. They burst in the oil, so fill only three quarters full."],"faq":[["Why is my samosa pastry soft, not crisp?","The dough was too soft, or the frying heat was too high. Knead firm, rest the dough, and fry low and slow."],["Can I make samosas in an air fryer?","Yes. Brush with oil and air fry at 180C for 12 to 15 minutes, turning once. The crust is crisp, though a little drier than deep fried."],["How do I stop the samosa opening while frying?","Seal with water and press firmly, and let the shaped samosas rest for 10 minutes before frying, so the seal sets."],["Can I use ready made pastry?","You can use spring roll sheets in a pinch, but the flavour and texture are different. The classic crisp comes from the firm flour dough in this recipe."]]},"bunkebab":{"intro":"Bun kebab is Karachi street food with a loyal following, a soft bun, a fried shami kebab, sliced onion and tomato, and chutneys that bring heat, tang and freshness in every bite. It is fast food made at home in 25 minutes, provided the shami kebabs are ready, and it is best eaten hot, straight off the tawa, with the bun lightly toasted and still soft. The balance matters more than any single ingredient. Too much chutney drowns the kebab, too little leaves the bun dry, and a well toasted bun holds everything together without falling apart in your hands.","ing":["Shami kebabs 8","buns 4","Onion","tomato","green chutney","Tamarind chutney","yoghurt","Green chilli","fresh coriander","Butter for toasting the bun"],"steps":["Slice the onion into thin rings, slice the tomato, and keep the chutneys, chillies and coriander ready in small bowls.","Heat a little oil on a tawa on medium heat. Fry the shami kebabs for 2 to 3 minutes per side, until hot through and lightly crisp at the edges.","Remove the kebabs and keep them warm. Wipe the tawa lightly, add butter, and toast the cut sides of the buns until lightly golden, about 1 minute.","Spread green chutney on the bottom bun and tamarind chutney on the top bun. A thin layer is enough, the kebab should still be the star.","Place 2 kebabs on each bottom bun. If the kebabs are small, overlap them slightly so every bite has kebab.","Add onion rings, tomato slices, green chilli and coriander. Add a spoon of whisked yoghurt if you like a cooler, creamier bite.","Close the bun and press gently. The chutney should just reach the edges, not drip out.","Serve hot at once, with extra chutney on the side and a cold drink. Eat with both hands, Karachi style."],"tip":"Do not over-toast the bun. Lightly golden is enough, or it will become hard and crumbly, and the kebab will slide out instead of sitting snugly inside.","notes":["Shami kebabs should be soft inside and lightly crisp outside. If they are very soft, chill them for 15 minutes before frying, so they hold their shape.","Use soft burger buns, not crusty rolls. The bun should squash gently around the kebab, not fight it.","Tamarind chutney gives the sweet sour note. Taste it first, because some are very sweet and need less.","Onion rings stay crisp if sliced just before serving. Sliced onion left sitting turns soft and sharp."],"variations":["Chicken bun kebab: use chicken shami kebabs and add a little extra green chutney, because chicken is milder than beef.","Egg bun kebab: add a fried egg on top of the kebab, with the yolk just set, for a richer street style version.","Spicy bun kebab: add sliced green chillies and a spoon of chilli garlic sauce, and skip the yoghurt."],"serving":"Serves 4, with 1 bun kebab per person, extra chutney, and a simple salad or fries on the side. Bun kebab is street food, so serve it wrapped in paper or on a plate, hot from the tawa, and eat it at once. It pairs well with a cold lassi, a fizzy drink, or chai. For a larger crowd, set out the buns, kebabs and chutneys and let people build their own.","storage":"Bun kebab is best eaten fresh and does not store well once assembled, because the bun soaks up the chutney. Keep the parts separate. Cooked shami kebabs keep in the fridge for up to 2 days and reheat on a tawa. Chutneys keep in the fridge for up to 4 days in sealed jars. Toast and assemble just before serving, so the bun stays soft and the kebab stays hot.","mistakes":["Over-toasting the bun. It turns hard, cracks, and the filling falls out.","Drowning the kebab in chutney. The balance is lost and the bun goes soggy in minutes.","Frying kebabs on very high heat. The outside burns while the centre stays cold.","Assembling too early. The bun absorbs moisture and the crisp edges soften before anyone eats."],"faq":[["What is a shami kebab?","A soft kebab made from minced meat and lentils, cooked, ground and shaped, then fried. It is soft enough to squash in a bun, unlike a firm seekh kebab."],["Can I use frozen shami kebabs?","Yes. Fry them straight from frozen on medium heat, giving 1 to 2 extra minutes per side, until hot through. Do not thaw them first, because soft kebabs break."],["Which bun is best?","A soft, plain burger bun. Sesame buns also work. Avoid hard rolls, because they crack and the filling falls out."],["Can I make it without tamarind chutney?","Yes. Use extra green chutney and a squeeze of lemon for tang. The flavour is different, but it still works well."]]},"gulabjamun":{"intro":"Gulab jamun is the sweet that ends most celebrations, soft, warm, and soaked in rose scented syrup until it is heavy with it. The secret is not a long list of ingredients. It is khoya that is fresh and soft, a dough handled gently, frying on low heat so the jamuns cook through without darkening too fast, and syrup that is warm, not boiling, when they go in. Rush any part and the jamuns turn hard, cracked, or dry inside. Take your time, and they swell, soften, and shine. Serve them warm, with the syrup spooned over, and watch them disappear.","ing":["Khoya 250g","flour 3 tbsp","Cardamom powder 1/2 tsp","Sugar 2 cups","water 2 cups","Rose water 1 tsp","2 drops of lemon","Oil / ghee for frying"],"steps":["Crumble the khoya and knead it with the flour and cardamom until a soft, smooth dough forms. Do not over knead, or the jamuns turn dense.","Cover the dough and rest for 15 minutes. It should be soft enough to roll without cracking.","Make the syrup by heating sugar, water and lemon juice until the sugar dissolves. Simmer for 5 minutes, add cardamom and rose water, and keep it warm, not boiling.","Grease your palms lightly with ghee. Roll small, smooth balls, about the size of a walnut, with no cracks on the surface.","Heat oil or ghee on low to medium low heat. The oil should be warm enough to sizzle gently, not hot enough to brown at once.","Add the jamuns in batches, stirring gently with the spoon so they roll and brown evenly, for 6 to 8 minutes, until lightly golden.","Lift the jamuns out and put them straight into the warm syrup. They will sizzle softly and begin to swell.","Rest for at least 1 hour, turning once, so the jamuns drink the syrup evenly. Serve warm, with syrup spooned over."],"tip":"The jamuns should have no cracks. Grease your hands lightly with ghee while shaping the balls. The syrup should not be too hot, or the jamuns will harden instead of soaking, and it should not be cold, or they will not absorb it.","notes":["Fresh, soft khoya makes soft jamuns. Dry, crumbly khoya needs a spoon of milk to bring the dough together.","Flour is a binder only. Too much flour makes hard jamuns, so measure it and do not add extra to fix a sticky dough, rest it instead.","Lemon juice in the syrup stops it crystallising as it cools, so the syrup stays clear and smooth.","Rose water goes in off the heat, so its scent stays bright instead of boiling away."],"variations":["Kesar jamun: soak a pinch of saffron in warm milk, add it to the syrup, and garnish with pistachios.","Stuffed jamun: press a small piece of dried fruit and nut mix into the centre of each ball before frying.","Jamun with ice cream: serve warm jamuns over vanilla ice cream, with warm syrup spooned on top, for a hot and cold dessert."],"serving":"Serves 6, with 3 to 4 jamuns per person in small bowls, with plenty of syrup. Serve warm, garnished with chopped pistachios if you like, and keep extra syrup in a jug for second helpings. Gulab jamun suits Eid, weddings and dinner parties, and it is best eaten with a spoon, straight from the bowl. A cup of chai after it is the classic finish.","storage":"Keep in the syrup in a covered container in the fridge for up to 4 days. Reheat gently in the syrup on low heat, or in the microwave for 20 to 30 seconds, until warm, not hot. Do not boil them in the syrup, because they break apart. Freeze without syrup for up to 1 month, thaw in the fridge, and warm in fresh syrup before serving.","mistakes":["Frying on high heat. The outside darkens fast and the inside stays raw and dense.","Shaping balls with cracks. Syrup gets in unevenly and the jamuns split or turn rough.","Putting jamuns into boiling syrup. The surface seals and they stay hard inside.","Over kneading the dough. It turns tough and the jamuns lose their softness."],"faq":[["Why are my gulab jamuns hard?","The dough was over kneaded, too much flour was used, or the syrup was too hot. Knead gently, measure the flour, and keep the syrup warm, not boiling."],["Can I use milk powder instead of khoya?","Yes. Mix 1 cup of milk powder with 2 tbsp of ghee and 3 to 4 tbsp of warm milk into a soft dough, then follow the same steps. The texture is close, though khoya is softer."],["Why did my jamuns break in the syrup?","They were fried too little, the dough was too soft, or the syrup was boiling. Fry until lightly golden and firm, and keep the syrup warm."],["Can I make the syrup less sweet?","You can reduce the sugar to 1½ cups, but the syrup must still cover the jamuns. Very thin syrup does not soak in the same way, so do not reduce it much further."]]},"gajarhalwa":{"intro":"Gajar ka halwa is winter in a bowl, red carrots cooked slowly in milk until the milk disappears, then fried in ghee with sugar and finished with khoya, cardamom and nuts. It is not a quick dessert, and that is the point. The long, gentle cooking turns grated carrot into something soft, rich and deeply flavoured, with no shortcuts that taste the same. Use red carrots when you can find them, grate them finely, and keep the heat low. Serve it warm, with a spoon of khoya on top, and save some for the next day, because it tastes even better then.","ing":["Red carrots 1 kg (grated)","Milk 1 litre","sugar 1 cup","Ghee 4 tbsp","khoya 150g","Cardamom 4","almonds / pistachios"],"steps":["Wash, peel and grate the carrots finely. Fine grating matters, because thick shreds take longer and stay coarse.","Put the carrots and milk in a heavy kadhai. Bring to a boil, then reduce to low heat.","Cook uncovered on low heat for 35 to 45 minutes, stirring every few minutes, until the milk has almost dried up.","Add the ghee and fry the carrots, stirring, for 8 to 10 minutes, until the mixture turns glossy and leaves the sides.","Add the sugar and cook for 8 to 10 minutes more, stirring, until the sugar melts and the halwa thickens again.","Crush the cardamom and add it with the khoya. Cook for 5 minutes, stirring, until the khoya melts into the halwa.","Taste and adjust. If it is too sweet, a squeeze of lemon is not traditional, so instead serve smaller portions with unsweetened chai.","Serve warm, topped with chopped almonds and pistachios and a little extra khoya."],"tip":"Grate the carrots finely. Thick carrots take longer to cook and the halwa stays coarse, no matter how long you stir it, so take the time to grate well at the start.","notes":["Red carrots are sweeter and give the deep colour. Orange carrots work, but the halwa is paler and needs a little more sugar.","Full fat milk gives a richer result. Low fat milk takes longer to reduce and tastes thinner.","Khoya at the end adds richness and body. It is worth buying good khoya, or crumbling paneer as a substitute in a pinch.","Cardamom should be freshly crushed. Pre ground cardamom loses its aroma quickly."],"variations":["Milk powder halwa: replace half the milk with 1 cup of milk powder mixed into the carrots, for a faster, richer version.","Nutty halwa: add chopped cashews and raisins with the khoya, and toast the nuts in ghee first.","Less sweet halwa: reduce the sugar to 3/4 cup. The halwa is still sweet from the carrots, and suits serving with ice cream."],"serving":"Serves 5, in small bowls, warm, with chopped nuts and a spoon of khoya on top. Gajar ka halwa is a winter dessert, served after dinner or with afternoon chai. A scoop of vanilla ice cream on warm halwa is a popular modern serving, and the contrast of hot and cold works beautifully. Keep portions small, because it is rich.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 4 days. Reheat in a pan on low heat with a splash of milk, stirring, until warm through. It also reheats well in the microwave in short bursts. Freeze for up to 1 month in portions, thaw in the fridge, and reheat with a little milk. Add fresh nuts at serving, because stored nuts soften.","mistakes":["Cooking on high heat. The milk catches and burns, and the halwa tastes scorched.","Adding sugar too early. It draws out water and the carrots boil instead of reducing, so add it after the milk dries.","Grating thickly. The halwa stays stringy and coarse, however long it cooks.","Skipping the ghee frying stage. Without it, the halwa tastes boiled, not rich and glossy."],"faq":[["Can I use orange carrots?","Yes. The halwa is paler and a little less sweet, so taste and add 1 to 2 tbsp more sugar if needed. Red carrots are traditional for winter halwa."],["How do I know the milk has reduced enough?","The mixture looks dry, the carrots hold together, and you see ghee starting to separate at the edges when you stir. That is the time to add the ghee and fry."],["Can I make it without khoya?","Yes. It is still good, just less rich. Add 2 extra tbsp of ghee and 2 tbsp of milk powder at the end, or crumble in soft paneer."],["Is gajar ka halwa served hot or cold?","Warm is best, straight from the kadhai or gently reheated. Cold halwa from the fridge is firm and less fragrant, so warm it before serving."]]},"lassi":{"intro":"Mango lassi is summer in a glass, ripe mangoes blended with cold yoghurt, a little milk, and cardamom, until it is thick, smooth and cold enough to cool you down. It takes 15 minutes, most of which is peeling the mango, and it needs no cooking at all. The quality of the mango decides everything, so choose fruit that smells sweet at the stem, gives slightly when pressed, and has no green, hard patches. Serve it at once, in chilled glasses, with a little chopped mango on top, and drink it while the blender foam is still on top.","ing":["Ripe mangoes 2","yoghurt 1½ cups","Milk 1/2 cup","sugar 2 tbsp (to taste)","Cardamom powder 1/4 tsp","Ice cubes","mint for garnish"],"steps":["Chill the glasses in the fridge for 15 minutes, so the lassi stays cold while you drink it.","Peel the mangoes, cut the flesh away from the stone, and chop it roughly. Taste a piece and decide how much sugar you need.","Put the mango, yoghurt, milk, sugar and cardamom in a blender. Do not add the ice yet.","Blend for 30 seconds, stop, scrape down the sides, and blend for 30 seconds more, until completely smooth.","Add a handful of ice cubes and blend for 15 to 20 seconds more, just until the ice is crushed and the lassi is thick and cold.","Taste and adjust. Add a little more sugar if the mango is tart, or a splash of milk if it is too thick to pour.","Pour into the chilled glasses at once, leaving the foam on top.","Garnish with chopped mango and a mint leaf, and serve at once with a straw or a spoon."],"tip":"The sweeter the mango, the less sugar you need. Do not make lassi in advance. Freshly blended is best, because it separates and loses its foam if it sits, and the ice melts and thins it.","notes":["Use ripe, sweet mangoes like Sindhri, Chaunsa or Alphonso when in season. Fibrous mangoes make a stringy lassi, so strain it if needed.","Full fat yoghurt gives a thick, creamy lassi. Low fat yoghurt works, but the lassi is thinner and needs less milk.","Cardamom should be a background note, not the main flavour. A quarter tsp is enough for 3 glasses.","Chill everything first. Cold mango, cold yoghurt and cold milk mean less ice is needed, and the flavour stays strong."],"variations":["Mango banana lassi: add 1 ripe banana for a thicker, sweeter lassi that suits children.","Mango rose lassi: add 1 tsp of rose water and a pinch of saffron, and garnish with rose petals.","Vegan lassi: use coconut yoghurt and oat milk, and sweeten with maple syrup or sugar to taste."],"serving":"Serves 3, in tall chilled glasses, with chopped mango and mint on top. Mango lassi suits hot afternoons, spicy meals, and breakfast on a summer weekend. Serve it as a drink with paratha and pickle, or as a light dessert after a spicy curry. Keep it cold until the moment it is poured, and drink it fresh.","storage":"Drink fresh for the best texture. Lassi keeps in a sealed jar in the fridge for up to 1 day, but it separates, so stir or shake well before serving, and do not expect the foam to return. Do not freeze lassi, because the yoghurt turns grainy on thawing. If you must make it ahead, blend the mango and yoghurt without ice, refrigerate, and add ice and blend again just before serving.","mistakes":["Using unripe mango. No amount of sugar fixes the sour, hard flavour, so wait for ripe fruit.","Over blending with ice. The lassi turns watery and foamy in a thin way, instead of thick and creamy.","Adding too much milk at the start. It thins the lassi before you can judge it, so add milk a little at a time.","Making it hours ahead. It separates, the colour dulls, and the fresh mango taste fades."],"faq":[["Can I use frozen mango?","Yes. Thaw it halfway, so it is soft enough to blend but still cold, and reduce the ice by half. Frozen mango makes a very thick lassi, so add milk a splash at a time."],["Is mango lassi healthy?","It is made from fruit and yoghurt, with no numbers added here. The sugar is the part to watch, so use ripe mangoes and add as little sugar as the fruit needs."],["Can I make lassi without a blender?","Yes, if the mango is very soft. Mash the mango with a fork, whisk in the yoghurt and milk, and pass it through a sieve. It is less smooth, but still tasty."],["Why is my lassi stringy?","The mango was fibrous. Blend longer, or pass the lassi through a fine sieve before serving. Next time, choose a less fibrous mango variety."]]},"pizza":{"intro":"Margherita pizza is proof that simple food can be extraordinary. It was made famous in Naples, where a hot oven, a thin crust, bright tomato sauce and good mozzarella do all the work. The colours are said to echo the Italian flag, red tomato, white mozzarella and green basil, and the flavour is clean and fresh rather than heavy. This version uses a dough that rests long enough to become light and chewy, a sauce that is barely cooked, and a very hot oven so the crust blisters in minutes. Take time with the dough and the heat, and the pizza will taste like a pizzeria pizza, even from a home oven.","ing":["Strong bread flour 500g","warm water 325ml","Instant yeast 7g","fine salt 10g","olive oil 2 tbsp","San Marzano tomatoes 400g (crushed)","Fior di latte mozzarella 250g (torn)","Fresh basil leaves 1 handful","Garlic 1 clove","olive oil","salt for the sauce","Semolina or flour for dusting"],"steps":["Mix the flour, water, yeast and salt, knead for 8 to 10 minutes until smooth, then rest the dough covered for 1 hour, or overnight in the fridge for better flavour.","Crush the tomatoes with garlic, a pinch of salt and 1 tbsp of olive oil. Do not cook the sauce, it cooks on the pizza.","Divide the dough into 4 balls, rest for 20 minutes, then stretch each one by hand from the centre out, leaving a thicker edge.","Heat the oven as hot as it will go, at least 250C, with a pizza stone or an upside down baking tray inside for 30 minutes.","Spread 3 to 4 tbsp of sauce on each base, leaving the edge clear, and scatter the torn mozzarella over the top.","Bake for 6 to 8 minutes until the crust is blistered and golden and the cheese is bubbling.","Add the basil leaves as soon as the pizza comes out, so they wilt gently without burning.","Drizzle with a little olive oil, slice, and serve at once while the crust is crisp."],"tip":"Do not overload the pizza. A thin layer of sauce and a modest amount of cheese let the crust cook through and stay crisp.","notes":["Bread flour gives the chew that plain flour cannot, so use it if you can find it, and weigh the flour for a reliable dough.","Fior di latte is the soft mozzarella sold in water. Drain and tear it, and pat it dry, so the pizza does not turn wet.","San Marzano tomatoes are sweet and low in acid. If you use another tin, crush them well and add a pinch of sugar only if they taste sharp.","Semolina on the peel stops the dough sticking and adds a little crunch. Flour alone can burn and taste bitter at high heat."],"variations":["Marinara: skip the cheese, add extra garlic and oregano, and finish with olive oil, for a dairy free pizza.","Four cheese: replace the tomato sauce with a thin layer of olive oil, then use mozzarella, gorgonzola, fontina and parmesan.","With cherry tomatoes: roast halved cherry tomatoes with olive oil first, then use them instead of the crushed tomato sauce."],"serving":"Serves 4, with one pizza per person, or cut into slices for sharing with a green salad. Serve straight from the oven, with extra basil, chilli oil or parmesan on the table, and let everyone add their own. Pizza is at its best in the first 10 minutes, when the base is crisp and the cheese is still soft and stretchy.","storage":"Leftover slices keep in the fridge for up to 2 days in an airtight container. Reheat in a hot oven or a dry frying pan with a lid, so the base crisps again. Avoid the microwave, it makes the crust soft and chewy. Raw dough keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days, and freezes well for up to 1 month, thawed overnight in the fridge.","mistakes":["Using a cool oven. The crust steams instead of blistering, so preheat the stone or tray fully.","Rolling the dough with a rolling pin. It presses out the air, so stretch it by hand instead.","Adding too much sauce or cheese. The centre stays wet and the base never crisps.","Putting basil on before baking at very high heat. It blackens, so add it after baking."],"faq":[["Why is my pizza base soggy in the middle?","The oven or stone was not hot enough, or there was too much sauce and cheese. Preheat fully, use less topping, and bake on the lowest shelf."],["Can I make the dough ahead?","Yes. After kneading, refrigerate it for up to 3 days. Bring it back to room temperature for 30 to 45 minutes before stretching."],["Do I need a pizza stone?","No. An upside down heavy baking tray, preheated in the oven, works well. A cast iron pan also gives a crisp base."],["Can I use dried basil?","Fresh basil is much better here, because it is the main flavour. Dried basil in the sauce is fine, but add fresh leaves to finish."]]},"carbonara":{"intro":"Real carbonara has no cream. It comes from Rome, and its sauce is made only from eggs, pecorino cheese, black pepper and the fat from guanciale or pancetta, mixed with hot pasta until it turns glossy and silky. The trick is heat control. The pan must be hot enough to cook the eggs gently into a sauce, but never so hot that they scramble. Work quickly, use pasta water to loosen the sauce, and serve at once. Once you learn the timing, this becomes one of the fastest and most comforting pasta dishes you can make, with only a handful of ingredients and a depth of flavour that tastes far more complex than it is.","ing":["Spaghetti 400g","Guanciale or pancetta 150g (diced)","Egg yolks 4","plus 1 whole egg","Pecorino Romano 60g (finely grated)","Black pepper 2 tsp (freshly cracked)","Salt for the pasta water","Extra pecorino","pepper to serve"],"steps":["Boil the spaghetti in well salted water until just al dente, and keep 1 cup of the pasta water before draining.","Fry the guanciale or pancetta in a dry pan on medium heat for 6 to 8 minutes until crisp at the edges and the fat has melted out.","Whisk the yolks, whole egg, pecorino and most of the black pepper into a thick paste. Add a spoon of pasta water if it is too stiff.","Take the pan off the heat and let it cool for 1 minute, so the eggs will not scramble when they meet the pan.","Add the drained spaghetti to the pan and toss well in the rendered fat for 30 seconds.","Pour in the egg and cheese mix and toss fast and constantly, adding pasta water a splash at a time until the sauce is glossy and coats every strand.","Taste, add more pepper, and add salt only if needed, because pecorino and pancetta are already salty.","Serve at once in warm bowls, with extra pecorino and black pepper on top."],"tip":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the eggs. Residual heat makes the sauce, direct heat makes scrambled eggs.","notes":["Pecorino Romano is sharp and salty, and it is the traditional cheese. Parmesan works, but it is milder, so use a little more and taste before adding salt.","Guanciale is cured pork cheek and gives the classic flavour. Pancetta is the closest easy substitute, and bacon is acceptable, though smokier.","Pasta water is full of starch. It helps the egg sauce cling to the spaghetti instead of sliding off.","Freshly cracked black pepper matters here. It is a main flavour, not just a garnish, so crack it coarse and use plenty."],"variations":["With peas: add a handful of peas to the pasta water in the last minute, for colour and sweetness.","Vegetarian style: replace the pork with mushrooms fried in butter and olive oil, and add a little smoked paprika, though the result is a different dish.","Lighter version: use 2 whole eggs and 2 yolks instead of 4 yolks, and a little less cheese, for a sauce that is still silky."],"serving":"Serves 4 as a main course, in warm bowls, with a simple green salad and crusty bread on the side. Carbonara is a one bowl meal, so keep the sides light and let the pasta be the star. Serve it the moment it is tossed, because the sauce thickens as it cools and loses its silkiness.","storage":"Carbonara is best eaten fresh and does not reheat well, because the egg sauce thickens and can split. If you must keep it, refrigerate for up to 1 day and reheat very gently in a pan with a splash of water, stirring constantly. Do not freeze it. Cooked pasta on its own keeps for 2 days, but make the sauce fresh.","mistakes":["Adding the eggs to a pan that is still on the heat. The eggs scramble and the sauce turns lumpy.","Using cream. It makes a different, heavier sauce and hides the pecorino flavour.","Draining all the pasta water. Without it, the sauce is too thick and will not coat the pasta.","Adding salt before tasting. Pecorino and cured pork are salty, so taste first."],"faq":[["Can I use bacon instead of guanciale?","Yes. Fry it until crisp, and drain a little of the fat if there is a lot. The flavour is smokier, but the method is the same."],["Why did my sauce scramble?","The pan was too hot. Take it off the heat, cool it for a minute, and toss quickly with pasta water next time."],["Can I make carbonara without pork?","Yes, with mushrooms or peas, but it will not taste like classic carbonara. Keep the egg and pecorino sauce the same."],["How do I know the pasta water amount?","Start with a small splash and add more as you toss. The sauce should look glossy and loose, not thick and pasty."]]},"sushi":{"intro":"Sushi is about rice first and fish second. In Japan, the rice is seasoned with vinegar, sugar and salt, fanned cool, and handled gently so every grain stays separate and glossy. This home version makes salmon and cucumber rolls, the kind that are easiest to learn, with nori on the outside, rice in the middle, and a neat line of filling. Use sushi grade salmon, keep your hands wet, and roll firmly but gently. The first roll may look uneven, and that is normal. By the third roll, the shape, the cut and the confidence all come together, and the result tastes fresh, clean and far better than shop bought sushi that has sat in a fridge.","ing":["Sushi rice 2 cups (uncooked)","Rice vinegar 4 tbsp","sugar 2 tbsp","salt 1 tsp","Nori sheets 4","Sushi grade salmon 300g (cut into strips)","Cucumber 1 (cut into thin sticks)","Mayonnaise 3 tbsp","sriracha 1 tsp (for spicy mayo)","Soy sauce","pickled ginger","wasabi to serve","Sesame seeds 1 tbsp (optional)"],"steps":["Rinse the rice until the water runs clear, cook it, then rest it covered for 10 minutes.","Warm the vinegar, sugar and salt until dissolved, fold it into the hot rice with a slicing motion, and fan the rice until it is just warm and glossy.","Lay a nori sheet shiny side down on a bamboo mat, wet your hands, and spread a thin even layer of rice, leaving 2cm bare at the far edge.","Spread a thin line of spicy mayo across the centre, add salmon and cucumber sticks in a neat row, and sprinkle with sesame seeds.","Lift the near edge of the mat and roll firmly away from you, tucking the filling in, and press gently to seal the bare edge with a little water.","Rest the roll for 2 minutes, then cut it with a wet sharp knife into 8 pieces, wiping the blade between cuts.","Repeat with the remaining rice and filling, keeping your hands wet throughout.","Arrange the pieces cut side up, and serve with soy sauce, pickled ginger and a small amount of wasabi."],"tip":"Keep a bowl of water nearby and wet your hands and knife often. Wet hands stop the rice sticking, and a wet blade cuts cleanly without squashing the roll.","notes":["Sushi rice is short grain rice. Long grain rice does not stick in the same way, so do not substitute it if you want neat rolls.","Sushi grade means fish sold for raw eating, kept cold from catch to sale. Ask your fish seller, and do not use ordinary fish that is meant for cooking.","Rice vinegar is mild and sweet. Do not replace it with strong malt vinegar, which would overpower the rice.","Nori should be crisp and dark. Store it sealed and dry, because damp nori tears and will not roll neatly."],"variations":["Tuna rolls: replace the salmon with sushi grade tuna, and add avocado instead of cucumber.","Cooked prawn rolls: use cooked prawns, cucumber and a little mayo, a good choice if you prefer no raw fish.","Vegetable rolls: use cucumber, avocado, carrot sticks and pickled radish, with sesame seeds, for a fully vegetarian roll."],"serving":"Serves 4 as a light meal, with 2 rolls per person, or as part of a larger Japanese meal with miso soup and salad. Serve at room temperature, soon after cutting, with soy sauce for dipping, pickled ginger to cleanse the palate between pieces, and wasabi used sparingly. Sushi is finger food, so set out chopsticks and small dishes, and let everyone serve themselves.","storage":"Sushi is best eaten the day it is made, within a few hours of rolling. Refrigeration dries the rice and dulls the fish, so do not make it a day ahead. If you must keep it, cover tightly and refrigerate for up to 6 hours, then bring it back to room temperature for 20 minutes before serving. Do not freeze sushi. Cooked sushi rice on its own keeps for 1 day in the fridge.","mistakes":["Using hot rice. It steams the nori soft and makes the roll fall apart, so cool the rice until just warm.","Overfilling the roll. Too much filling stops the roll closing, so keep the filling to one neat row.","Cutting with a dry knife. The rice sticks and drags, so wet the blade before every cut.","Pressing too hard while rolling. The rice squashes and the filling squeezes out, so roll firmly but gently."],"faq":[["Is raw salmon safe to eat at home?","Only if it is sushi grade salmon from a trusted seller, kept cold, and eaten the same day. When in doubt, use cooked prawns or vegetables instead."],["Can I make sushi without a bamboo mat?","Yes. Use a tea towel under cling film, or a sheet of baking paper. The mat makes it easier, but it is not essential."],["Why is my rice sticky and mushy?","It was not rinsed enough, or it was stirred hard while seasoning. Rinse well and fold the seasoning in gently."],["Can I prepare the rice ahead?","Yes, by a few hours. Keep it covered at room temperature, not in the fridge, and roll just before serving."]]},"ramen":{"intro":"Ramen is comfort in a bowl, and a good home version does not need a broth that simmers all day. This chicken ramen builds flavour quickly, with garlic, ginger and spring onions fried in sesame oil, chicken stock enriched with soy and miso, and noodles cooked separately so the broth stays clear and the noodles stay springy. The egg is the finishing touch, soft in the middle and seasoned by the broth as you eat. Set out the toppings in small bowls and let everyone build their own bowl, hot broth last, so the noodles do not sit and soften. It is fast, warming and deeply satisfying, and it tastes like care, not like a shortcut.","ing":["Chicken breasts or thighs 500g","Chicken stock 1.5 litres","Ramen noodles 4 portions","Garlic 4 cloves","ginger 3cm piece","Soy sauce 3 tbsp","miso paste 2 tbsp","Sesame oil 1 tbsp","spring onions 4","Eggs 4 (soft boiled)","Chilli oil","sesame seeds","nori to serve"],"steps":["Boil the eggs for 6 and a half minutes, cool in cold water, peel, and halve them just before serving.","Heat sesame oil in a large pot, fry the garlic, ginger and the white parts of the spring onions for 2 minutes until fragrant.","Pour in the chicken stock, add soy sauce, and simmer gently for 15 minutes so the flavours come together.","Cook the chicken in the simmering broth for 12 to 15 minutes until cooked through, then lift it out, slice it, and keep it warm.","Whisk the miso with a ladle of hot broth until smooth, then stir it back into the pot. Do not boil hard after adding miso.","Cook the noodles separately according to the packet, drain well, and divide between 4 warm bowls.","Ladle the hot broth over the noodles, top with sliced chicken, egg halves, spring onion greens and sesame seeds.","Serve at once with chilli oil and nori on the side, and eat while the broth is steaming hot."],"tip":"Cook the noodles separately. Noodles cooked in the broth soak it up, turn the broth cloudy, and go soft before you serve.","notes":["Miso adds depth and salt. White miso is mild and sweet, red miso is stronger, so start with less if you use red.","Fresh ramen noodles cook in 2 to 3 minutes and stay springy. Dried noodles work too, but check the packet time and do not overcook them.","Ginger should be sliced, not minced, so it flavours the broth without making it cloudy or gritty.","Sesame oil is for flavour, not for frying at high heat. Add it at the start on medium heat, and do not let it smoke."],"variations":["Spicy ramen: stir 1 tbsp of chilli paste or gochujang into the broth with the miso.","Vegetable ramen: replace the chicken with mushrooms and tofu, and use vegetable stock, keeping the same aromatics.","Pork ramen: use thinly sliced cooked pork instead of chicken, and add sweetcorn and bean sprouts on top."],"serving":"Serves 4, in deep bowls, with chopsticks and a soup spoon, and the toppings in the middle of the table. Serve the broth steaming hot, with extra chilli oil, soy sauce and sesame seeds to hand, so each person can season their own bowl. Ramen is eaten at once, while the noodles are springy and the egg is still warm, so call everyone to the table before you ladle the broth.","storage":"Broth keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days, and freezes well for up to 1 month. Noodles and toppings do not keep well once combined, so store them separately and assemble fresh. Reheat the broth until boiling, cook fresh noodles, and add the toppings just before serving. Leftover sliced chicken keeps for 2 days in the fridge.","mistakes":["Boiling the broth hard after adding miso. It dulls the flavour and can split, so keep it at a gentle simmer.","Overcooking the noodles. They soften in the hot broth, so cook them just until springy and drain well.","Adding the egg too early. It overcooks in the hot broth, so add the halved egg at the very end.","Serving in cold bowls. The broth cools too fast, so warm the bowls with hot water first."],"faq":[["Can I use instant noodles?","Yes, but discard the flavour sachet and use this broth instead. Cook the noodle block separately and drain it well."],["Can I make the broth ahead?","Yes. Make it up to 3 days ahead and refrigerate. Reheat until boiling, then add miso fresh and assemble the bowls."],["How do I get a jammy egg?","Boil a fridge cold egg for 6 and a half minutes, cool at once in cold water, and peel gently. The yolk should be soft and just set."],["Is ramen very salty?","It can be. Taste before adding extra soy sauce, because stock, soy and miso all add salt."]]},"tacos":{"intro":"Tacos al pastor began with Lebanese shawarma cooking in Mexico City, and it became something entirely Mexican, pork marinated in dried chillies and achiote, cooked until charred at the edges, and served in warm corn tortillas with pineapple, onion and coriander. The marinade does the heavy work, so give it time, even 30 minutes makes a difference, and overnight is better. Cook the pork hot and fast so it chars without drying out, warm the tortillas over an open flame or in a dry pan, and keep the toppings simple. The sweet pineapple against the smoky, spiced pork is the whole point, so do not skip it, and serve with lime and salsa on the side.","ing":["Pork shoulder 700g (thinly sliced)","Dried guajillo chillies 3","ancho chilli 1","Achiote paste 2 tbsp","garlic 3 cloves","White vinegar 3 tbsp","orange juice 60ml","Cumin 1 tsp","oregano 1 tsp","salt","Pineapple 300g (cut into chunks)","Corn tortillas 12","white onion 1","coriander","Limes 2","salsa of your choice"],"steps":["Soak the dried chillies in hot water for 15 minutes until soft, then drain, keeping a little of the soaking water.","Blend the chillies, achiote, garlic, vinegar, orange juice, cumin, oregano and salt into a smooth marinade, adding soaking water as needed.","Coat the pork in the marinade, cover, and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes, or overnight for the best flavour.","Heat a large pan or griddle on high heat with a little oil, and cook the pork in batches for 3 to 4 minutes per side until charred and cooked through.","In the same hot pan, sear the pineapple chunks for 1 to 2 minutes per side until golden at the edges.","Chop the pork into small pieces and toss with the pineapple and a spoon of the pan juices.","Warm the tortillas in a dry pan or over a flame until soft and lightly spotted, and keep them wrapped in a cloth.","Fill each tortilla with pork and pineapple, top with diced onion and coriander, and serve with lime wedges and salsa."],"tip":"Cook in batches and do not crowd the pan. Crowded pork steams instead of charring, and you lose the crisp edges that make al pastor special.","notes":["Achiote paste gives the red colour and earthy flavour. Find it in Mexican shops or online, and do not replace it with food colour alone.","Guajillo chillies are mild and fruity. Ancho adds depth. Remove the seeds if you want less heat, and wear gloves when handling dried chillies.","Pork shoulder stays juicy when cooked hot and fast. Lean pork loin dries out more easily, so slice it thinner and cook it for less time.","Corn tortillas are traditional. Flour tortillas work, but they are softer and less able to hold the juicy filling."],"variations":["Chicken al pastor: use chicken thighs with the same marinade, and cook until charred, for a lighter version.","With avocado: add sliced avocado and a squeeze of lime for creaminess, keeping the pineapple as well.","Spicier version: add 1 chipotle in adobo to the marinade, and serve with a hot salsa on the side."],"serving":"Serves 4, with 3 tacos per person, plus lime, salsa, extra onion and coriander on the table. Serve the tacos as soon as they are filled, while the tortillas are warm and the pork is still sizzling, and let everyone add their own lime and salsa. A simple side of rice and beans, or a crisp cabbage salad, turns the tacos into a full meal without taking attention from them.","storage":"Marinated raw pork keeps in the fridge for up to 1 day. Cooked pork keeps for up to 3 days in the fridge, and reheats best in a hot pan with a splash of water, so it crisps again. Tortillas keep wrapped at room temperature for 1 day, or in the fridge for 3 days, and warm up in a dry pan. Do not store assembled tacos, they go soggy. Pineapple keeps separately for 2 days.","mistakes":["Using a cool pan. The pork boils in its marinade instead of charring, so get the pan smoking hot first.","Skipping the marinade time. Even 30 minutes matters, and pork marinated for only 5 minutes tastes flat.","Overfilling the tortillas. They split and the filling falls out, so use 2 to 3 tbsp of pork per taco.","Forgetting the lime. The acid lifts the rich pork, so always serve lime wedges."],"faq":[["Can I grill the pork instead?","Yes. Grill over high heat for 3 to 4 minutes per side, then chop and toss with pineapple. A griddle pan gives the closest result indoors."],["What if I cannot find achiote paste?","Use 1 tbsp of smoked paprika, 1 tsp of ground cumin and a pinch of turmeric for colour. The flavour is different, but it still works."],["Are corn tortillas gluten free?","Pure corn tortillas made only from corn, water and lime are naturally gluten free, but check the packet for cross contamination if that matters to you."],["Can I make the marinade ahead?","Yes. Blend it and refrigerate for up to 3 days, or freeze it for 1 month. Marinate the pork on the day you cook it."]]},"pozole":{"intro":"Pozole is a celebration soup from Mexico, made with hominy, the large white corn kernels that puff and soften in a long simmer, and pork that cooks until it shreds with a spoon. The red version gets its colour and depth from dried guajillo and ancho chillies, blended smooth and stirred into the broth, while the toppings stay fresh and crisp, shredded cabbage, radish, onion, oregano and lime. It takes time, but most of that time is unattended, and the pot feeds a crowd. Cook it on a weekend, keep the broth rich and the toppings separate, and let everyone finish their own bowl at the table.","ing":["Pork shoulder 1kg (cut into large chunks)","White hominy 800g","drained (rinsed)","Dried guajillo chillies 4","ancho chillies 2","Onion 1","garlic 6 cloves","Bay leaves 2","oregano 2 tsp","cumin 1 tsp","Salt","water or light stock 3 litres","Shredded cabbage","radish","onion","lime to serve","Dried oregano","chilli flakes to serve"],"steps":["Soak the dried chillies in hot water for 15 minutes, drain, and blend with garlic and a little soaking water until smooth.","Put the pork, hominy, onion, bay leaves and water in a large pot, bring to a boil, and skim off any foam.","Reduce to a gentle simmer, cover partly, and cook for 90 minutes, topping up with hot water if the level falls too low.","Strain the blended chilli sauce through a sieve into the pot, stir well, and season with salt, cumin and oregano.","Simmer for 30 more minutes until the pork is tender enough to shred and the broth is rich and red.","Lift out the pork, shred it with two forks, and return it to the pot. Taste and adjust the salt and chilli.","Prepare the toppings while the pozole finishes, shredded cabbage, thin radish slices, diced onion, lime wedges and oregano.","Ladle into deep bowls, add a generous amount of toppings, and serve with warm tortillas on the side."],"tip":"Strain the chilli sauce before adding it. The sieve catches skins and seeds, and the broth stays smooth and silky instead of gritty.","notes":["Hominy is corn treated with lime, which makes the kernels large, soft and easy to digest. Canned hominy is ready to use, dried hominy needs overnight soaking and longer cooking.","Guajillo chillies give colour and a mild fruity heat. Ancho adds a darker, raisin like depth. Together they make the classic red broth.","Pork shoulder has enough fat to stay juicy through a long simmer. Lean pork dries out, so keep the chunks large and the simmer gentle.","Skim the foam at the start. It keeps the broth clear and the flavour clean."],"variations":["Green pozole: replace the red chillies with tomatillos, green chillies and coriander, blended and added in the same way.","Chicken pozole: use bone in chicken thighs instead of pork, and reduce the simmering time to about 45 minutes.","Vegetarian pozole: use mushrooms and beans with vegetable stock, and add the same chilli sauce and toppings."],"serving":"Serves 6, in deep bowls, with the toppings in separate bowls in the middle of the table. Each person adds cabbage for crunch, radish for bite, onion, oregano and a good squeeze of lime, which lifts the rich broth. Serve with warm corn tortillas for scooping, and keep extra chilli flakes on the side for those who want more heat.","storage":"Pozole keeps in the fridge for up to 4 days, and the flavour improves overnight. Store the broth and pork together, and the toppings separately. Reheat gently until boiling, adding a splash of water if it has thickened. It freezes well for up to 3 months, thawed overnight in the fridge. Do not freeze the fresh toppings.","mistakes":["Boiling hard the whole time. The pork toughens and the broth turns cloudy, so keep a gentle simmer.","Adding the chilli sauce without straining. Skins and seeds make the broth gritty.","Serving without fresh toppings. They are not decoration, they balance the rich broth with crunch and acid.","Under salting the broth. Taste at the end, because hominy and pork need a good amount of salt to shine."],"faq":[["Can I use a pressure cooker?","Yes. Cook the pork and hominy at pressure for 35 to 40 minutes, then finish with the chilli sauce in an open pot for 10 minutes."],["Is pozole very spicy?","The red version is warm, not fiercely hot. Remove chilli seeds for a milder broth, and let chilli flakes at the table add heat."],["Can I use dried hominy?","Yes, but soak it overnight and simmer it until tender before adding the pork, which can take an extra hour."],["What do I serve with pozole?","Warm corn tortillas, and the fresh toppings listed here. A simple salad is not needed, the toppings are the salad."]]},"padthai":{"intro":"Pad thai is Thailand's most famous noodle dish, and its magic is balance, sweet, sour, salty and a little heat, all in one hot wok. The sauce is made from tamarind, fish sauce, palm sugar and chilli, and it coats rice noodles that are soaked, not boiled, so they stay springy instead of soft. Prawns, egg, bean sprouts and chives go in quickly, at high heat, and the dish is finished with crushed peanuts, lime and extra sprouts on the side. Have everything ready before you start, because once the wok is hot, the cooking takes only a few minutes, and that speed is what keeps the noodles from turning mushy.","ing":["Flat rice noodles 250g","Prawns 300g (peeled)","Eggs 2 (lightly beaten)","Tamarind paste 3 tbsp","fish sauce 3 tbsp","Palm sugar or brown sugar 3 tbsp","Garlic 3 cloves","chilli flakes 1 tsp","Bean sprouts 2 cups","garlic chives or spring onions","Roasted peanuts 4 tbsp (crushed)","lime 1"],"steps":["Soak the rice noodles in warm water for 20 to 30 minutes until pliable but still firm, then drain well.","Mix the tamarind, fish sauce, sugar and chilli flakes into a sauce, and taste it. It should be sweet, sour and salty at once.","Heat oil in a wok on high heat, fry the garlic for 20 seconds, then add the prawns and cook for 2 minutes until just pink.","Push the prawns to one side, pour in the eggs, and scramble them gently until just set, then mix with the prawns.","Add the drained noodles and the sauce, and toss constantly for 2 to 3 minutes until the noodles are tender and the sauce is absorbed.","Add half the bean sprouts and the chives, toss for 30 seconds, and take the wok off the heat.","Divide between plates, top with the remaining sprouts, crushed peanuts and spring onions.","Serve at once with lime wedges, extra chilli flakes and fish sauce on the side, for each person to adjust."],"tip":"Soak the noodles, do not boil them. Boiled noodles overcook in the wok and turn soft and sticky.","notes":["Tamarind paste gives the sour note that defines pad thai. If it is very thick, thin it with a little warm water before mixing the sauce.","Palm sugar melts into a caramel like sweetness. Brown sugar works well, but white sugar is sharper, so use a little less.","Fish sauce is salty and pungent. Add it to the sauce, not directly to the wok, so it blends evenly.","Flat rice noodles vary in thickness. Thinner noodles soak faster, so check them after 15 minutes and drain while still firm."],"variations":["Chicken pad thai: replace the prawns with thinly sliced chicken, cooked through before the eggs go in.","Tofu pad thai: use firm tofu, fried until golden first, for a vegetarian version, and check that your fish sauce substitute suits you.","Extra vegetables: add shredded carrot and shredded cabbage with the noodles, for more crunch and colour."],"serving":"Serves 4, on warm plates, with lime wedges, crushed peanuts, extra bean sprouts and chilli flakes on the table. Pad thai is a street food dish, so serve it hot and casual, with everyone squeezing lime over their own plate and adding more peanuts or heat as they like. A light cucumber salad on the side is refreshing, but keep the focus on the noodles.","storage":"Pad thai is best eaten fresh, straight from the wok. Leftovers keep in the fridge for up to 1 day, and reheat in a hot wok or pan with a splash of water, tossing constantly. The noodles soften on reheating, so do not expect the same texture. Do not freeze it. The sauce on its own keeps in the fridge for up to 1 week.","mistakes":["Overcooking the noodles. They should be firm when drained, because they finish cooking in the sauce.","Using a wok that is not hot enough. The noodles steam and stick, so heat the wok until it just starts to smoke.","Adding all the sprouts too early. They wilt and go soft, so add half at the end and keep half raw for topping.","Forgetting to taste the sauce. Balance it before cooking, because you cannot fix sweet, sour and salty once the noodles are in."],"faq":[["Can I make pad thai without fish sauce?","Yes, use soy sauce with a pinch of salt, or a vegetarian fish sauce. The flavour is different, but the balance of sweet and sour stays."],["What if my noodles stick together?","They were soaked too long or the wok was not hot enough. Drain well, toss with a little oil, and cook in a hotter wok next time."],["Can I use dried shrimp?","Yes. Soak 2 tbsp of dried shrimp and add them with the garlic. They add a traditional salty depth."],["Is pad thai gluten free?","Rice noodles are naturally gluten free, but fish sauce and tamarind paste can contain additives, so check the labels."]]},"pekingduck":{"intro":"Peking duck is one of China's great celebration dishes, famous for skin so crisp it shatters, and meat that stays juicy underneath. The secret is drying the skin and glazing it before roasting, so the fat renders out and the skin turns a deep lacquered red gold. This home version uses an overnight rest in the fridge to dry the skin, a simple maltose and vinegar glaze, and a hot oven, followed by a rest before carving. Serve it the traditional way, in thin mandarin pancakes with hoisin sauce, spring onion and cucumber, and let everyone assemble their own. It is a project, not a weeknight dinner, and it is worth every minute.","ing":["Whole duck 2 to 2.5kg","Maltose or honey 3 tbsp","rice vinegar 2 tbsp","Shaoxing wine 2 tbsp","five spice 1 tsp","Salt 1 tbsp","boiling water for blanching","Mandarin pancakes 12","Hoisin sauce 120ml","Spring onions 4","cucumber 1","Ginger","garlic for the cavity (optional)"],"steps":["Clean the duck, pat it very dry, and prick the skin lightly all over with a fork, taking care not to pierce the meat.","Pour boiling water over the duck in the sink, which tightens the skin, then pat dry again.","Warm the maltose, vinegar, Shaoxing wine, five spice and salt until the maltose dissolves, and brush it all over the duck.","Rest the duck uncovered in the fridge overnight, on a rack over a tray, so the skin dries completely.","Roast breast side up at 200C for 30 minutes, then reduce to 180C and roast for 60 to 75 minutes more, until the skin is deep golden and crisp.","Baste once halfway with the remaining glaze, and cover the wing tips with foil if they brown too fast.","Rest the duck for 20 minutes before carving, so the juices settle and the skin stays crisp.","Carve the skin and meat into thin slices, warm the pancakes, and serve with hoisin, spring onion batons and cucumber sticks."],"tip":"Dry the skin overnight. Wet skin steams in the oven and never crisps, while dry skin renders and lacquers beautifully.","notes":["Maltose gives the classic shine and a mild sweetness. Honey works, but it browns faster, so watch the duck in the last 20 minutes.","Five spice is strong. Measure it, and do not add extra, or it will overpower the duck.","A duck has a lot of fat under the skin. Pricking the skin lets it render out, and roasting on a rack keeps the duck out of its own fat.","Mandarin pancakes are thin and soft. Steam them for 2 minutes just before serving, and keep them covered so they do not dry out."],"variations":["With shop bought duck: use a roast duck from a Chinese deli, crisp the skin in a hot oven, and serve the same way with pancakes and hoisin.","Duck breast version: score duck breasts, sear skin side down until crisp, finish in the oven, and slice thinly for pancakes.","With plum sauce: replace hoisin with plum sauce for a sweeter, fruitier finish."],"serving":"Serves 6, as the centre of a shared meal, with the carved duck on a platter, warm pancakes, hoisin, spring onion and cucumber in the middle of the table. Each person spreads hoisin on a pancake, adds a few slices of duck and skin, a spring onion baton and cucumber, and rolls it up. Serve with a light soup or stir fried greens on the side, and keep the pancakes coming while the duck lasts.","storage":"Leftover duck keeps in the fridge for up to 2 days, stored in an airtight container. Reheat the meat gently in a covered pan with a splash of water, and crisp the skin separately under a hot grill or in a hot oven for a few minutes. Pancakes keep in the fridge for 2 days and reheat by steaming. Do not freeze the carved skin, it loses its crispness.","mistakes":["Skipping the overnight drying. The skin stays soft and fatty, no matter how hot the oven is.","Roasting at too low a temperature. The fat does not render, so the skin is greasy instead of crisp.","Carving too soon. The juices run out and the skin steams, so rest for a full 20 minutes.","Using thick pancakes. They overpower the duck, so use thin mandarin pancakes and warm them well."],"faq":[["Can I make Peking duck in a smaller oven?","Yes, if the duck fits with air around it. A smaller duck of about 2kg is easier, and the method is the same."],["What if I cannot find maltose?","Use honey thinned with a little hot water. The glaze will be a little darker, so watch it near the end of roasting."],["Can I use duck legs only?","Yes. Dry and glaze them the same way, and roast for about 50 to 60 minutes until crisp. They are easier to handle than a whole duck."],["Why is my duck skin not crisp?","The skin was not dry enough, or the oven was too cool. Dry it overnight, prick it well, and roast hot."]]},"dimsum":{"intro":"Dim sum means food that touches the heart, and it is meant to be shared, small bamboo steamers arriving at the table one after another, each with a different bite inside. This home version makes two classics, prawn har gow with translucent wrappers, and pork and prawn siu mai with open tops, both steamed until just cooked and served hot. The wrappers need a light touch and a little patience, but the filling is simple, and the steaming is quick. Make them with help, one person filling, one person folding, and keep the finished dumplings covered so they do not dry out. Serve with tea, as in Cantonese yum cha, and let the meal stretch out slowly.","ing":["Prawns 300g (finely chopped)","Pork mince 200g","Wheat starch 120g","tapioca starch 60g (for har gow)","Wonton wrappers 20 (for siu mai)","Ginger 1 tbsp (grated)","spring onions 2","Soy sauce 2 tbsp","sesame oil 1 tsp","white pepper","Sugar 1 tsp","Shaoxing wine 1 tbsp","Chilli oil","soy sauce","black vinegar to serve"],"steps":["Mix the prawns, pork, ginger, spring onions, soy, sesame oil, sugar, wine and white pepper into a filling, and chill for 20 minutes.","For har gow wrappers, mix wheat starch and tapioca starch with 180ml of just boiled water, knead into a smooth dough, and rest covered for 15 minutes.","Roll the har gow dough into a log, cut into small pieces, and press each one into a thin round with the side of a cleaver or a rolling pin.","Place a spoon of filling in each wrapper, fold into a crescent, and pleat the edge firmly, sealing the top.","For siu mai, place a spoon of filling in the centre of each wonton wrapper, gather the edges up around the filling, and press gently so the top stays open.","Line bamboo steamers with baking paper with holes, or cabbage leaves, and arrange the dumplings with space between them.","Steam har gow for 6 to 7 minutes and siu mai for 8 minutes, until the wrappers are translucent or set and the filling is cooked.","Serve at once in the steamers, with chilli oil, soy sauce and black vinegar for dipping, and hot tea on the side."],"tip":"Do not overfill the dumplings. A level teaspoon of filling seals neatly, while too much filling bursts the wrapper in the steamer.","notes":["Wheat starch makes har gow wrappers translucent and delicate. Plain flour will not give the same result, so buy wheat starch from an Asian shop.","Keep the dough covered at all times. It dries in minutes and cracks when you try to fold it.","Chill the filling before shaping. Cold filling is easier to portion and holds its shape in the wrapper.","Steam in batches if needed. Crowded steamers stick together, and the dumplings tear when you lift them."],"variations":["Vegetable dim sum: replace the meat with finely chopped mushrooms, cabbage, carrot and tofu, seasoned the same way.","Chicken dim sum: use chicken mince with prawns, and add water chestnuts for crunch.","With shop bought wrappers: use round dumpling wrappers for both types, pleated closed, for an easier version that still tastes excellent."],"serving":"Serves 4, as part of a shared meal, with 5 to 6 dumplings per person, plus tea, and a simple cucumber salad or stir fried greens on the side. Serve the steamers straight to the table, lid on, so the dumplings stay hot and steamy until the moment they are eaten. Dim sum is slow food in small bites, so refill the tea, bring the steamers out in batches, and do not rush.","storage":"Uncooked dumplings freeze well for up to 1 month on a tray, then bagged. Steam from frozen for 3 to 4 minutes longer. Cooked dumplings keep in the fridge for 1 day, and reheat by steaming for 3 minutes, not in the microwave, which toughens the wrappers. Leftover filling keeps for 1 day in the fridge, covered.","mistakes":["Using wrappers that are too thick. They stay doughy after steaming, so roll or press them thin.","Steaming on a dry steamer. The dumplings stick and tear, so line the steamer and oil it lightly.","Opening the lid constantly. The steam escapes and the dumplings cook unevenly, so keep the lid on until the time is up.","Making the dumplings too far ahead. The wrappers dry out and crack, so shape them just before steaming, or keep them covered with a damp cloth."],"faq":[["Can I make dim sum without a bamboo steamer?","Yes. Use a metal steamer, a colander over a pot, or a plate on a rack in a covered wok. Line it well so nothing sticks."],["Why are my har gow wrappers cracking?","The dough dried out, or it cooled too much. Keep it covered, work with small pieces, and knead it briefly if it stiffens."],["Can I use frozen prawns?","Yes. Thaw them fully, pat them dry, and chop them just before mixing. Wet prawns make a loose filling."],["What sauce goes with dim sum?","Chilli oil, light soy sauce and black vinegar, mixed to taste. A little grated ginger in the vinegar is traditional."]]},"coqauvin":{"intro":"Coq au vin is classic French comfort food, chicken braised slowly in red wine until it falls from the bone, with mushrooms, pearl onions and bacon in a sauce that tastes of the wine without tasting alcoholic. It comes from Burgundy, where the wine is part of the cooking, not just an accompaniment, and the long gentle braise is what makes the chicken tender and the sauce rich. Brown the chicken well first, because that colour becomes flavour, then let the pot do the work in the oven. Serve it with mashed potatoes or crusty bread, and give the sauce a final taste for salt and a little butter to make it shine.","ing":["Chicken thighs","legs 1kg (bone in)","Red wine 500ml (Burgundy style if possible)","Bacon lardons 150g","Mushrooms 250g","pearl onions 12","Carrot 1","garlic 3 cloves","thyme 3 sprigs","Chicken stock 250ml","tomato paste 1 tbsp","Flour 2 tbsp","butter 2 tbsp","Parsley to serve","salt","pepper"],"steps":["Season the chicken well, dust lightly with flour, and brown it in batches in a hot ovenproof pot until deep golden on both sides. Set aside.","Fry the bacon in the same pot until crisp, lift it out, and brown the mushrooms and pearl onions in the bacon fat.","Add the carrot, garlic and tomato paste, and cook for 2 minutes, stirring, until fragrant.","Pour in the wine, scraping the bottom of the pot to lift all the browned flavour, and simmer for 3 minutes.","Return the chicken and bacon to the pot, add stock and thyme, and bring to a gentle simmer. The liquid should come halfway up the chicken.","Cover and cook in the oven at 160C for 75 to 90 minutes, until the chicken is very tender.","Lift out the chicken, and simmer the sauce on the stove for 10 minutes until it coats a spoon. Whisk in the butter at the end.","Return the chicken to the sauce, warm through, and serve scattered with parsley."],"tip":"Brown the chicken properly before braising. Pale chicken makes a pale sauce, while deep golden chicken makes a rich, full flavoured sauce.","notes":["Bone in chicken stays juicy through a long braise. Boneless chicken cooks faster and can dry out, so reduce the oven time if you use it.","Use a wine you would drink. Cheap cooking wine makes a harsh sauce, while a fruity red wine gives depth and colour.","Pearl onions are small and sweet. If you cannot find them, use shallots cut into quarters.","Butter whisked in at the end gives the sauce a soft shine and rounds off the wine. Do not boil the sauce after adding it."],"variations":["White wine version: use dry white wine and add cream at the end, for a lighter coq au vin blanc.","With potatoes: add small new potatoes to the pot for the last 40 minutes, so they cook in the sauce.","Slow cooker version: brown everything first, then cook on low for 5 to 6 hours, and reduce the sauce on the stove at the end."],"serving":"Serves 4, in shallow bowls, with creamy mashed potatoes, buttered noodles or crusty bread to catch the sauce. The chicken should be served with a generous ladle of sauce, mushrooms and onions, and a scattering of parsley. This is a Sunday dish, so serve it at the table from the pot, with a simple green salad on the side and the rest of the wine, if you like, in glasses.","storage":"Coq au vin keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days, and tastes even better the next day. Reheat gently on the stove until bubbling, adding a splash of stock if the sauce is too thick. It freezes well for up to 3 months, thawed overnight in the fridge. Store the chicken in its sauce, so it does not dry out.","mistakes":["Skipping the browning. The sauce will lack colour and depth, so take the time to brown in batches.","Boiling the braise hard. The chicken toughens and the sauce reduces too fast, so keep a gentle simmer and a low oven.","Using too much wine and no stock. The sauce turns sharp, so balance wine with stock and taste before serving.","Adding the butter too early. It splits in a long simmer, so whisk it in at the very end, off the boil."],"faq":[["Can I make coq au vin without alcohol?","Yes, replace the wine with extra stock plus 2 tbsp of red wine vinegar and a little grape juice. The flavour is different, but the braise still works."],["Can I use chicken breast?","You can, but add it for only the last 30 minutes, or it will dry out. Thighs and legs are much better for this dish."],["What wine should I use?","A dry, fruity red wine, ideally from Burgundy. Avoid very tannic or oaky wines, which can turn bitter in a long braise."],["Can I make it ahead?","Yes, and it improves. Cook it fully, cool, refrigerate, and reheat gently the next day."]]},"bouillabaisse":{"intro":"Bouillabaisse comes from Marseille, where fishermen made soup from the fish that did not sell, and it is now one of France's most loved celebration dishes. The broth is the star, built from fish stock, tomatoes, fennel, orange peel, saffron and olive oil, and the fish and shellfish cook in it for only a few minutes, so they stay tender. Serve it in two parts if you can, broth strained into bowls and fish on a platter, with rouille, a saffron garlic mayonnaise, spread on toasted bread. It sounds grand, but the method is simple, make a good broth, add the fish in the right order, and do not overcook it.","ing":["Mixed firm white fish 800g (cut into chunks)","Prawns 300g","mussels 300g (cleaned)","Fish stock 1.5 litres","Tomatoes 400g (chopped)","tomato paste 2 tbsp","Fennel 1 bulb","onion 1","leek 1","Saffron 1 large pinch","orange peel 1 strip","Garlic 4 cloves","olive oil 4 tbsp","thyme","Crusty bread","rouille","parsley","lemon to serve"],"steps":["Heat olive oil in a large pot, fry the onion, leek, fennel and garlic for 8 minutes until soft and sweet.","Add the tomato paste and chopped tomatoes, and cook for 5 minutes until the tomatoes start to break down.","Pour in the fish stock, add saffron, orange peel, thyme and a pinch of salt, and simmer for 20 minutes.","Strain the broth through a sieve, pressing the vegetables to get all the flavour, then return the broth to the pot.","Bring the broth back to a gentle simmer, and add the firmest fish first, cooking for 3 minutes.","Add the remaining fish and the prawns, and cook for 3 to 4 minutes more, until just cooked through.","Add the mussels, cover, and cook for 3 minutes until they open. Discard any that stay closed.","Ladle the broth and seafood into warm bowls, and serve with toasted bread spread with rouille, parsley and lemon."],"tip":"Do not overcook the fish. Add the firmest pieces first and the delicate pieces last, and take the pot off the heat as soon as the fish flakes.","notes":["Saffron is expensive, but a pinch transforms the broth with colour and aroma. Soak it in a spoon of hot stock for 10 minutes before adding it.","Use a mix of fish, such as monkfish, sea bass and red mullet if you can find them. One fish alone makes a thinner soup.","Orange peel adds a bright, fragrant note that is classic in Marseille. Use a wide strip with no white pith, and remove it before serving.","Rouille is made by whisking egg yolk, garlic, saffron, lemon and olive oil into a mayonnaise. Shop bought aioli with saffron is a fair shortcut."],"variations":["With clams: add cleaned clams with the mussels, and discard any that do not open.","Simpler weeknight version: use only prawns and one firm white fish, with the same broth, and serve with bread.","With potatoes: add diced potatoes to the broth and simmer until tender before adding the fish, for a heartier soup."],"serving":"Serves 6, in deep warm bowls, with the fish and shellfish arranged on top, and a plate of rouille topped toast in the middle of the table. Each person spoons rouille into the broth or spreads it on bread, and squeezes lemon over the fish. Serve with a green salad and a glass of dry white wine, and keep the bread coming, because the broth is too good to leave.","storage":"Bouillabaisse is best eaten fresh, the day it is made. The broth on its own keeps in the fridge for up to 2 days and freezes well for 1 month. Leftover fish does not reheat well and can turn rubbery, so store the broth separately and add fresh fish when you reheat. Reheat the broth until boiling, then cook the seafood fresh in it.","mistakes":["Overcooking the seafood. Fish and prawns go from tender to tough in a minute, so watch the pot closely.","Skipping the straining step. The broth stays chunky and the flavour is less clear, so strain and press the vegetables.","Using oily fish. Salmon and mackerel overpower the delicate broth, so use firm white fish.","Adding all the fish at once. Delicate fish breaks up while firm fish is still cooking, so add in stages."],"faq":[["Can I use shop bought fish stock?","Yes. Choose a good quality stock, and simmer it with the vegetables and saffron as written. It will still taste rich."],["What is rouille?","A saffron and garlic mayonnaise from Provence, spread on toast and stirred into the soup. It adds richness and colour."],["Can I make bouillabaisse without shellfish?","Yes, use only fish, and add a little extra fish stock. The soup is still excellent, though less varied."],["Is saffron essential?","It gives the classic colour and aroma. Without it, the soup is still good, but it is not quite bouillabaisse."]]},"paella":{"intro":"Paella comes from Valencia in Spain, where it is cooked outdoors in a wide shallow pan, and the rice, not the seafood, is the real star. Saffron gives it the golden colour, a good stock gives it flavour, and the last few minutes of cooking, left undisturbed, create socarrat, the crisp toasted layer of rice at the bottom that everyone fights over. Use short grain bomba rice if you can, spread it in an even layer, and resist stirring once the stock goes in. Serve it in the pan, in the middle of the table, with lemon wedges, and let everyone dig in together.","ing":["Bomba or short grain rice 400g","Prawns 300g","mussels 300g (cleaned)","Squid rings 200g (optional)","Chicken or fish stock 1 litre (hot)","Saffron 1 large pinch","Onion 1","red pepper 1","garlic 3 cloves","Tomatoes 2 (grated)","smoked paprika 1 tsp","Olive oil 4 tbsp","lemon 1","peas 100g"],"steps":["Soak the saffron in 3 tbsp of hot stock for 10 minutes, and keep the rest of the stock hot.","Heat olive oil in a wide paella pan, fry the onion and pepper for 6 minutes until soft, then add garlic and paprika.","Add the grated tomato and cook for 4 minutes until it darkens and thickens.","Add the rice and stir for 1 minute to coat it in the sofrito, then spread it in an even layer.","Pour in the hot stock and the saffron liquid, season with salt, and bring to a gentle boil. Do not stir from this point.","Cook on medium heat for 15 minutes, then arrange the seafood and peas on top, pressing them lightly into the rice.","Reduce the heat, cover loosely with foil, and cook for 10 minutes more, until the rice is tender and the mussels have opened.","Rest the pan covered for 5 minutes, then serve in the pan with lemon wedges, listening for the crackle of the socarrat."],"tip":"Do not stir the rice after the stock goes in. Stirring releases starch and makes the rice sticky, and it stops the socarrat forming.","notes":["Bomba rice absorbs a lot of liquid and stays separate. Long grain rice goes mushy in paella, so use short grain rice if bomba is not available.","Keep the stock hot. Cold stock cools the pan and the rice cooks unevenly.","Smoked paprika gives a warm, smoky note. Sweet paprika works, but hot paprika can overpower the saffron.","Saffron should be soaked before use, so its colour and aroma spread evenly through the rice."],"variations":["Chicken and chorizo paella: add browned chicken and sliced chorizo with the sofrito, and use chicken stock.","Vegetable paella: use vegetable stock, artichokes, green beans and butter beans, with the same saffron rice method.","Mixed paella: combine chicken, prawns and mussels, adding the chicken early and the seafood late, as in the main method."],"serving":"Serves 6, served in the pan in the middle of the table, with lemon wedges and a green salad on the side. Each person takes a portion from the edge inwards, so everyone gets some of the crisp bottom layer. Paella is a sharing dish, so set out extra lemon, crusty bread and aioli if you like, and do not serve it on individual plates in the kitchen.","storage":"Paella is best eaten fresh, straight from the pan. Leftovers keep in the fridge for up to 2 days, and reheat in a hot pan with a splash of stock, covered, until steaming. The rice will not be as crisp, and the seafood should not be reheated for long. Do not freeze paella with shellfish, the texture suffers. The socarrat is only there on the day.","mistakes":["Stirring the rice. It turns sticky and the bottom never crisps.","Using the wrong rice. Long grain rice and risotto rice both give the wrong texture.","Adding cold stock. The cooking stops and starts, and the rice cooks unevenly.","Overloading the pan. Too much seafood on top steams the rice instead of letting it toast, so keep the layer even and not too deep."],"faq":[["Do I need a paella pan?","A wide shallow pan works, as long as the rice layer is thin. A deep pot makes the rice too thick and it steams instead of toasting."],["Can I make paella without saffron?","You can, with a pinch of turmeric for colour, but the flavour is different. Saffron is the defining flavour of paella."],["How do I know the rice is done?","Taste it. It should be tender with a slight bite, and the liquid should be absorbed. Resting for 5 minutes finishes it."],["Why is my paella mushy?","The rice was stirred, the stock was cold, or the heat was too low. Keep the rice undisturbed and the heat steady."]]},"beefpho":{"intro":"Pho is Vietnam's most famous soup, and its heart is a clear broth that tastes of charred onion and ginger, star anise, cinnamon, cloves and fish sauce, simmered gently with beef bones until it is rich but never cloudy. The noodles cook separately, the beef is sliced paper thin and cooks in the hot broth as it is poured over, and the bowl is finished at the table with bean sprouts, Thai basil, coriander, lime and chilli. It takes time, but the work is mostly waiting and skimming, and the result is a bowl that is light, fragrant and deeply comforting, nothing like a heavy stew.","ing":["Beef bones 1.5kg","marrow or knuckle bones","Beef brisket or shin 500g","Rice noodles 500g (flat)","Onion 2","ginger 1 large piece","Star anise 3","cinnamon stick 1","cloves 4","Coriander seeds 1 tsp","fennel seeds 1 tsp","Fish sauce 4 tbsp","rock sugar 1 tbsp","Bean sprouts","Thai basil","coriander","lime","chilli to serve"],"steps":["Blanch the bones in boiling water for 5 minutes, drain, rinse, and return them to a clean pot with fresh cold water.","Char the onion halves and ginger over an open flame or under a hot grill until blackened in patches, then add to the pot.","Toast the star anise, cinnamon, cloves, coriander and fennel in a dry pan for 2 minutes until fragrant, tie in muslin or add loose and strain later.","Simmer the broth very gently for 2 and a half hours, skimming often, and add the brisket for the last 60 minutes until tender.","Lift out the brisket, cool slightly, slice thinly, and strain the broth through a fine sieve until clear.","Season the broth with fish sauce and rock sugar, tasting as you go, until it is savoury, fragrant and just a little sweet.","Cook the rice noodles separately, drain, and divide between bowls with slices of brisket and raw thin sliced beef.","Ladle the boiling broth over each bowl, so the raw beef cooks in the heat, and serve with herbs, sprouts, lime and chilli."],"tip":"Keep the simmer gentle and skim often. A rolling boil makes the broth cloudy and greasy, while a quiet simmer keeps it clear and clean tasting.","notes":["Charring the onion and ginger adds a smoky sweetness that is essential to pho. Do not skip it, and do not peel the onion fully, the skin adds colour.","Rock sugar balances the fish sauce without making the broth taste sweet. Ordinary sugar works, but use a little less.","Slice the raw beef while it is partly frozen. It slices thinly and evenly, and cooks in seconds in the hot broth.","Rice noodles should be soaked or cooked until just tender, then drained well, so they do not soak up all the broth in the bowl."],"variations":["Chicken pho: use a whole chicken and chicken bones, simmer for 60 to 75 minutes, and shred the meat for the bowls.","Vegetarian pho: use mushroom and vegetable stock with the same spices, charred onion and ginger, and tofu and mushrooms on top.","Spicier pho: add sliced fresh chilli and chilli sauce at the table, rather than in the broth, so each person controls the heat."],"serving":"Serves 6, in deep bowls, with the herbs, sprouts, lime and chilli in separate bowls in the middle of the table. Each person adds their own herbs and a squeeze of lime, and eats with chopsticks and a spoon, noodles first, then the broth. Pho is served steaming hot and eaten at once, while the herbs are still fresh and the beef is just cooked.","storage":"Broth keeps in the fridge for up to 4 days and freezes well for up to 3 months. Store the broth, noodles and toppings separately, and assemble fresh. Reheat the broth until boiling, cook fresh noodles, and slice the beef just before serving. Leftover cooked brisket keeps for 2 days in the fridge. Do not store assembled bowls.","mistakes":["Boiling the broth hard. It turns cloudy and the fat emulsifies into the soup, so keep it at a bare simmer.","Not skimming. Foam and fat left in the pot dull the flavour and the clarity.","Overcooking the noodles. They soak up the broth and turn mushy, so cook them separately and drain well.","Forgetting to taste the seasoning. Fish sauce and sugar must be balanced at the end, so season in small amounts and taste often."],"faq":[["Can I make pho in a pressure cooker?","Yes. Cook the bones at pressure for 60 to 75 minutes, then strain and season. The flavour is very close, though the broth is a little less clear."],["What beef is best for the raw slices?","Sirloin or eye of round, sliced very thin while partly frozen. It cooks in the hot broth in seconds."],["Is pho gluten free?","Rice noodles and most fish sauce are naturally gluten free, but check your fish sauce and hoisin labels for additives."],["Can I make the broth ahead?","Yes, and it improves overnight. Refrigerate, remove the solidified fat, and reheat until boiling."]]},"poutine":{"intro":"Poutine comes from Quebec in Canada, and it is gloriously simple, hot crisp fries, fresh cheese curds that squeak when you bite them, and a rich brown gravy poured over the top so the curds just start to melt. The fries must be crisp enough to hold the gravy without going soggy in a minute, so double frying is worth it, and the gravy must be piping hot, because it is the heat that softens the curds. Serve it at once, in a bowl, with a fork, and eat it while the fries are still crisp at the edges and the gravy is pooling at the bottom.","ing":["Potatoes 1kg (floury variety)","Cheese curds 250g (at room temperature)","Beef or vegetable stock 500ml","Butter 2 tbsp","flour 2 tbsp","Soy sauce 1 tsp","Worcestershire sauce 1 tsp","Garlic 1 clove","black pepper","Oil for deep frying","salt","Spring onions to serve (optional)"],"steps":["Cut the potatoes into thick batons, soak in cold water for 30 minutes, then drain and dry very well.","Heat oil to 160C, fry the chips in batches for 5 to 6 minutes until pale and soft, then drain and rest for 10 minutes.","While the chips rest, melt butter in a pan, stir in the flour, and cook for 2 minutes until lightly golden.","Whisk in the hot stock a little at a time, then add soy, Worcestershire, garlic and pepper, and simmer for 5 minutes until smooth and glossy.","Heat the oil to 190C, and fry the chips again in batches for 2 to 3 minutes until deep golden and very crisp. Drain and salt at once.","Pile the hot chips into warm bowls, scatter the cheese curds over the top, and keep the curds at room temperature so they soften, not melt away.","Pour the piping hot gravy over the top, so it runs into the gaps and starts to melt the curds.","Serve at once with a fork, with spring onions scattered over if you like, and eat while hot."],"tip":"Use cheese curds at room temperature. Cold curds stay firm and do not soften, while room temperature curds go soft and squeaky under the hot gravy.","notes":["Floury potatoes make the fluffiest chips. Waxy potatoes stay dense and do not crisp in the same way.","Cheese curds should be fresh and squeaky. If you cannot find curds, use torn pieces of fresh mozzarella, though the squeak will be missing.","Gravy should be thin enough to run, not thick like a stew. It thickens as it cools, so serve it very hot.","Double frying is the secret to crisp chips that last. The first fry cooks the inside, the second fry crisps the outside."],"variations":["With pulled pork: top with slow cooked pulled pork and extra gravy, for a heartier main course.","With mushrooms: add fried mushrooms and thyme to the gravy, and use vegetable stock, for a vegetarian version.","Breakfast poutine: add a fried egg on top, and serve with bacon on the side."],"serving":"Serves 4, as a snack, a side, or a sharing main course, in deep bowls with forks. Serve it the moment the gravy is poured, while the chips are crisp and the curds are softening, and do not let it sit, because the chips soften quickly. Poutine is casual food, so serve it in the middle of the table, with extra gravy in a jug for anyone who wants more.","storage":"Poutine is best eaten fresh and does not reheat well once assembled, because the chips go soft. Store the components separately, chips, curds and gravy, for up to 2 days in the fridge. Reheat the chips in a hot oven until crisp, reheat the gravy until boiling, and assemble fresh. Gravy on its own freezes well for 1 month.","mistakes":["Using cold cheese curds. They stay hard and do not melt into the fries.","Pouring lukewarm gravy. It does not soften the curds, so heat the gravy until it is steaming hot.","Frying the chips only once. They go soft under the gravy in minutes, so double fry them.","Salting the chips before the second fry. Salt draws out water and stops them crisping, so salt only at the end."],"faq":[["What are cheese curds?","Fresh pieces of curdled milk, pressed lightly, with a squeaky texture when bitten. They are the defining ingredient of poutine."],["Can I use frozen chips?","Yes. Cook them until very crisp in the oven or air fryer, then assemble with hot gravy and curds. Double frying fresh chips is better, but frozen chips work."],["Is poutine vegetarian?","With vegetable stock and vegetarian Worcestershire sauce it can be. Traditional gravy uses beef stock, so check the stock you use."],["Can I make the gravy ahead?","Yes. Make it up to 3 days ahead, refrigerate, and reheat until boiling before pouring over the chips."]]},"bobotie":{"intro":"Bobotie is South Africa's beloved baked mince dish, with roots in Cape Malay cooking, where spiced mince is sweetened with fruit, enriched with soaked bread, and baked under a golden egg custard topping with bay leaves. The flavour is warm and gently sweet, curry powder, turmeric, chutney, raisins and apricot all working together, and the texture is soft and comforting, somewhere between a savoury bake and a light meatloaf. Serve it with yellow rice, chutney and a simple salad, and let the sweet and savoury balance do the talking. It is an ideal make ahead dish for a crowd, because it reheats beautifully and tastes even better the next day.","ing":["Beef or lamb mince 750g","Onion 1","garlic 2 cloves","ginger 1 tsp","Curry powder 2 tbsp","turmeric 1 tsp","White bread 2 slices","milk 250ml","Eggs 3","bay leaves 4","Chutney 3 tbsp","raisins 3 tbsp","Dried apricots 4 (chopped)","lemon juice 1 tbsp","Almonds 2 tbsp (flaked)","salt","pepper"],"steps":["Soak the bread in 125ml of the milk until soft, then squeeze out the excess milk and crumble the bread.","Fry the onion, garlic and ginger in oil for 5 minutes until soft, then add the curry powder and turmeric and fry for 1 minute.","Add the mince and fry, breaking it up, until browned all over and the liquid has cooked off.","Stir in the soaked bread, chutney, raisins, apricots, lemon juice and seasoning, and cook for 5 minutes. Taste and adjust the sweet and sour balance.","Spoon the mince into a buttered baking dish, press it down evenly, and tuck the bay leaves into the top.","Whisk the remaining milk with 2 eggs and a pinch of salt, and pour it gently over the mince.","Bake at 180C for 35 to 40 minutes, until the topping is set and golden, and the edges are bubbling.","Rest for 10 minutes, scatter with flaked almonds, and serve with yellow rice and extra chutney."],"tip":"Taste the mince before baking. The sweet, sour and spicy balance must be right at this stage, because baking will not fix it.","notes":["Soaked bread keeps the mince soft and light. Without it, bobotie can turn dense and dry.","Curry powder varies a lot. Use a mild Cape Malay style curry powder if you can, and add more only after tasting.","Chutney adds sweetness and tang. Fruit chutney is traditional, and mango chutney works well too.","Bay leaves go on top, not mixed in, so they flavour the custard and look beautiful when served."],"variations":["With lentils: replace half the mince with cooked brown lentils, for a lighter version with the same spices.","Vegetarian bobotie: use lentils and mushrooms with the same fruit, chutney and egg topping.","Spicier version: add 1 chopped fresh chilli with the onion, and serve with extra chutney and sambal on the side."],"serving":"Serves 6, with yellow rice, fruit chutney and a crisp green salad or sliced tomato and onion salad. Serve it straight from the baking dish, in generous squares, with the golden topping and a bay leaf on each portion. Bobotie is a family dish, so put the dish in the middle of the table, with the rice and chutney alongside, and let everyone help themselves.","storage":"Bobotie keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days, covered, and reheats well in the oven at 160C until hot through. It also freezes well for up to 3 months, thawed overnight in the fridge. The egg topping can weep a little on reheating, so reheat gently and covered with foil. Yellow rice keeps separately for 2 days.","mistakes":["Using very lean mince without bread. It turns dry and crumbly, so keep the soaked bread in.","Overbaking the topping. It goes rubbery, so take it out as soon as it is set and golden.","Skipping the rest. The bobotie falls apart when cut hot, so rest for 10 minutes before serving.","Making it too sweet. Taste the mince, and balance chutney and fruit with lemon juice, not more sugar."],"faq":[["Can I make bobotie ahead?","Yes. Assemble it fully, refrigerate for up to 1 day, and bake when needed, adding 10 minutes to the baking time."],["Is bobotie spicy?","It is warmly spiced, not hot. The curry powder gives flavour, and the fruit and chutney keep it gentle."],["What is yellow rice?","Rice cooked with turmeric, raisins and sometimes cinnamon, served with bobotie in South Africa. Plain rice also works."],["Can I use lamb mince?","Yes. Lamb makes a richer bobotie. Drain any excess fat after browning, so the bake is not greasy."]]},"moussaka":{"intro":"Moussaka is Greece's great layered bake, soft aubergine, a rich tomato and lamb or beef mince sauce scented with cinnamon, and a thick bechamel top that turns golden in the oven. It takes a little time, because each layer is cooked separately before the dish is assembled, but that is what gives it its structure and flavour, and it is best made ahead and rested, so the layers set and slice cleanly. Serve it warm, in squares, with a simple salad, and do not rush the resting time. It is a Sunday dish, generous and comforting, and it feeds a table happily.","ing":["Aubergines 2 large (sliced)","Potatoes 3 (sliced, optional)","Lamb or beef mince 600g","Onion 1","garlic 3 cloves","Tomatoes 400g (chopped)","tomato paste 2 tbsp","Cinnamon 1 tsp","oregano 1 tsp","nutmeg","Milk 500ml","butter 50g","flour 50g","eggs 2","Cheese 80g (grated)","kefalotyri or parmesan"],"steps":["Salt the aubergine slices and rest for 30 minutes, then pat dry and fry or roast until golden and tender. Set aside.","Boil or roast the potato slices until just tender, if using, and set aside.","Fry the onion and garlic until soft, add the mince, and brown well, breaking it up.","Add the tomato paste, chopped tomatoes, cinnamon, oregano and seasoning, and simmer for 20 minutes until thick.","Make the bechamel by melting butter, stirring in flour, and whisking in warm milk until thick and smooth. Cool slightly, then whisk in the eggs and half the cheese.","Layer potatoes, then aubergine, then mince sauce in a buttered baking dish, repeating once, and finish with the bechamel.","Sprinkle with the remaining cheese and a little nutmeg, and bake at 180C for 40 to 45 minutes until golden and bubbling.","Rest for 20 minutes before slicing, so the layers set, and serve warm with salad."],"tip":"Rest the moussaka before cutting. Hot from the oven it falls apart, rested it slices into clean, beautiful layers.","notes":["Salting the aubergine draws out bitter water and stops it soaking up too much oil. Pat it dry before frying.","Cinnamon is the signature spice in Greek mince sauce. Use it lightly, so it warms the sauce without tasting sweet.","Bechamel must be thick enough to sit on top. If it is thin, it sinks into the mince, so cook it until it coats a spoon.","Grate the cheese finely so it melts evenly into the bechamel and browns nicely on top."],"variations":["Vegetarian moussaka: replace the mince with lentils and mushrooms, keeping the same spices and bechamel.","With courgette: add a layer of roasted courgette slices with the aubergine, for extra vegetables.","Lighter version: roast the aubergine instead of frying it, and use semi skimmed milk for the bechamel."],"serving":"Serves 6, in generous squares, with a simple Greek salad of tomato, cucumber, onion and olives, and crusty bread on the side. Serve it warm, not piping hot, so the flavours have settled and the layers hold. Moussaka is a sharing dish, so bring the baking dish to the table, and serve with extra salad and bread for anyone who wants more.","storage":"Moussaka keeps in the fridge for up to 3 days, covered, and reheats well in the oven at 160C until hot through. It freezes well for up to 3 months, in portions, thawed overnight in the fridge. Reheat covered with foil so the top does not overbrown. Do not microwave a whole dish, the bechamel can split.","mistakes":["Skipping the salting of aubergine. It stays bitter and soaks up oil, making the bake greasy.","Making a thin bechamel. It sinks and the top never sets golden.","Cutting too soon. The layers slide apart, so rest for at least 20 minutes.","Undercooking the mince sauce. A watery sauce makes the bake soggy, so simmer until thick."],"faq":[["Can I make moussaka ahead?","Yes, and it is better made ahead. Assemble it, refrigerate for up to 1 day, and bake when needed, adding 10 minutes."],["Can I use beef instead of lamb?","Yes. Beef makes a milder moussaka. Lamb is traditional and richer, so choose the one you prefer."],["Is moussaka gluten free?","Not as written, because of the flour in the bechamel. Use a gluten free flour blend for the bechamel, and check your stock."],["Why is my bechamel lumpy?","The milk was added too fast, or the flour was not cooked. Whisk constantly, add warm milk slowly, and cook until smooth."]]},"hummus":{"intro":"Good hummus is all about texture, silky, light and whipped, never grainy or heavy. It comes from the Levant, where chickpeas, tahini, lemon and garlic are blended with ice water until the dip turns pale and fluffy, then finished with olive oil, paprika and whole chickpeas. The secret is to blend the tahini first, on its own, until it is completely smooth, and to use warm chickpeas, or chickpeas with their skins rubbed off, for the smoothest result. Serve it as part of a mezze spread, with warm pitta, olives and salad, and let everyone scoop and share.","ing":["Chickpeas 400g (cooked)","drained","Tahini 120g (well stirred)","Lemon juice 3 tbsp","garlic 1 small clove","Ice water 4 to 6 tbsp","Olive oil 2 tbsp (plus more to serve)","Salt 1 tsp","cumin 1/2 tsp (optional)","Paprika","parsley to serve","Warm pitta bread to serve"],"steps":["If using tinned chickpeas, warm them in their liquid for 5 minutes, drain, and keep a little liquid. Rub off loose skins if you have time.","Blend the tahini, lemon juice, garlic and salt for 2 minutes until very smooth, pale and slightly fluffy.","Add the warm chickpeas and blend for 3 to 4 minutes, scraping down the sides, until completely smooth.","Add ice water a tablespoon at a time, blending after each, until the hummus is light, whipped and soft enough to scoop.","Taste and adjust the lemon, salt and garlic. It should be bright and savoury, with tahini flavour, not sharp.","Spoon into a shallow bowl, swirl the top with the back of a spoon, and drizzle with olive oil.","Top with a few whole chickpeas, a pinch of paprika and chopped parsley.","Serve at room temperature with warm pitta, vegetable sticks and olives."],"tip":"Blend the tahini first, before the chickpeas. Tahini blended alone turns pale and creamy, and that is what makes the hummus silky.","notes":["Tahini quality matters most. Use a runny, well stirred tahini made from hulled sesame seeds. Thick, dry tahini makes heavy hummus.","Ice water whips air into the hummus and loosens it without adding oil. Add it slowly, or the hummus turns runny.","Garlic should be small and fresh. One large raw clove can overpower the whole bowl, so start small.","Warm chickpeas blend smoother than cold ones, because their starch is soft. If using cold chickpeas, warm them first."],"variations":["Roasted red pepper hummus: blend in 1 roasted red pepper, and reduce the lemon a little.","Beetroot hummus: blend in 1 small cooked beetroot for colour and sweetness, a beautiful pink version.","Spicy hummus: add 1 tsp of harissa or chilli paste with the tahini, and top with chilli oil."],"serving":"Serves 6, as part of a mezze spread, with warm pitta, olives, pickles, salad and other dips. Serve the hummus in a wide shallow bowl, with the olive oil pooled in the swirl, and let everyone tear pitta and scoop. It is also excellent as a sandwich spread, a base for roasted vegetables, or a side with grilled meat and falafel.","storage":"Hummus keeps in the fridge for up to 4 days in an airtight container, with a thin layer of olive oil on top. Bring it back to room temperature before serving, and stir well. It freezes well for up to 3 months, thawed overnight in the fridge, then whisked smooth again. Do not leave it at room temperature for more than 2 hours.","mistakes":["Blending everything at once. The tahini never gets smooth, so blend it first on its own.","Using cold, dry chickpeas. They blend grainy, so warm them and add ice water slowly.","Adding too much garlic. Raw garlic grows stronger as hummus sits, so use a little and taste.","Serving straight from the fridge. Cold hummus is stiff and dull, so let it warm up first."],"faq":[["Can I use dried chickpeas?","Yes. Soak overnight, cook until very soft with a pinch of bicarbonate of soda, and use warm. They give the smoothest hummus."],["Why is my hummus grainy?","The tahini was not blended first, the chickpeas were cold, or the skins were left on. Blend longer and add ice water."],["Can I make hummus without tahini?","You can, with extra olive oil and a little sesame oil, but it will not taste like classic hummus. Tahini is the heart of the dish."],["Is hummus healthy?","It is made from chickpeas, tahini, lemon and olive oil, with no numbers added here. The oil and salt are the parts to watch, so serve it with vegetables and pitta."]]},"falafel":{"intro":"Real falafel is made from raw soaked chickpeas, never tinned or cooked chickpeas, and that is what gives it a light, fluffy inside and a crisp, craggy crust. The chickpeas are ground coarse with onion, garlic, parsley, coriander and cumin, rested so the mixture holds together, then shaped and fried until deep golden. Serve them in warm pitta with salad and tahini sauce, or as part of a mezze spread with hummus. The mixture can be made ahead and chilled, and the falafel fry in minutes, so they are perfect for a crowd, as long as you do not crowd the pan.","ing":["Dried chickpeas 250g (soaked overnight)","Onion 1","garlic 3 cloves","Parsley 1 large bunch","coriander 1 bunch","Cumin 2 tsp","coriander powder 1 tsp","Baking powder 1/2 tsp","flour 2 tbsp","Salt 1 tsp","black pepper","chilli flakes","Oil for frying","Pitta","salad","tahini sauce to serve"],"steps":["Soak the chickpeas in plenty of cold water overnight, then drain very well. Do not cook them.","Put the chickpeas, onion, garlic, herbs, spices, salt and pepper in a food processor, and pulse until coarse and crumbly, not a paste.","Transfer to a bowl, stir in the flour and baking powder, cover, and refrigerate for 30 minutes to firm up.","Test one falafel first, shape a small ball, fry it, and check that it holds together and is cooked through.","Shape the rest into small balls or patties, about the size of a walnut, with wet hands.","Heat oil to 175C, and fry in batches for 3 to 4 minutes, turning once, until deep golden and crisp all over.","Drain on a wire rack, not on paper, so the crust stays crisp, and sprinkle with a little salt.","Serve hot in pitta with salad, tomato, cucumber and tahini sauce, or on a mezze plate with hummus."],"tip":"Use raw soaked chickpeas, never cooked chickpeas. Cooked chickpeas make a soft, mushy falafel that falls apart in the oil.","notes":["Soaking overnight is essential. Under soaked chickpeas stay hard inside, so give them a full 12 hours in cold water.","Pulse, do not puree. A coarse texture with visible bits of herb gives falafel its light, fluffy bite.","Chilling the mixture helps it hold its shape. If it feels wet, add a spoon of flour, but do not add too much or the falafel turn heavy.","Oil temperature matters. Too cool and the falafel soak up oil, too hot and they brown outside while staying raw inside."],"variations":["Baked falafel: brush with oil and bake at 200C for 20 to 25 minutes, turning once, for a lighter version.","With broad beans: replace half the chickpeas with soaked split broad beans, for the Egyptian style taameya.","Spicy falafel: add 1 chopped fresh chilli and extra cumin to the mixture, and serve with chilli sauce."],"serving":"Serves 4, with 4 to 5 falafel per person, in warm pitta with salad and tahini, or as part of a mezze spread. Serve them the moment they come out of the oil, while the crust is crisp and the inside is hot and fluffy, with lemon wedges and extra tahini on the side. Falafel are finger food, so set out plenty of pitta, salad and sauces, and let everyone build their own.","storage":"Uncooked falafel mixture keeps in the fridge for up to 2 days, covered. Cooked falafel keep for 2 days in the fridge, and reheat in a hot oven until crisp, about 8 minutes. Do not microwave them, they go soft. Uncooked shaped falafel freeze well for 1 month, fried from frozen for a minute longer.","mistakes":["Using tinned chickpeas. They are already cooked, so the falafel turn mushy and fall apart.","Over processing the mixture. A smooth paste makes dense, heavy falafel, so pulse briefly and keep it coarse.","Frying in oil that is too cool. The falafel absorb oil and go greasy, so heat the oil properly first.","Shaping them too large. Large falafel stay raw inside, so keep them walnut sized."],"faq":[["Can I bake falafel instead of frying?","Yes. They are less crisp, but still tasty. Brush with oil, bake at 200C, and turn once halfway."],["Why did my falafel fall apart?","The mixture was too wet, or the oil was too cool. Chill the mixture, add a little flour, and test one first."],["Can I use tinned chickpeas if I add flour?","It is not recommended. Even with flour, cooked chickpeas make a dense falafel. Soaked raw chickpeas are essential."],["What sauce goes with falafel?","Tahini sauce thinned with lemon and water, plus salad in pitta. Chilli sauce and pickles are also traditional."]]},"feijoada":{"intro":"Feijoada is Brazil's national dish, a rich black bean stew with a mix of pork, sausage and smoked meats, cooked slowly until the beans are creamy and the meats are tender. It is traditionally served on Saturdays, as a long family lunch, with white rice, sauteed collard greens, farofa, toasted cassava flour, and orange slices to cut the richness. It takes time, and it feeds a crowd, so cook a big pot, keep the accompaniments simple, and let the meal stretch out. Soak the beans overnight, brown the meats well, and give the pot a long, gentle simmer, and the result is deeply satisfying.","ing":["Dried black beans 500g (soaked overnight)","Pork shoulder 500g (cubed)","Smoked sausage 300g (sliced)","Bacon or salt pork 200g (diced)","Onion 1","garlic 4 cloves","Bay leaves 3","cumin 1 tsp","oregano 1 tsp","Orange 1 (for serving)","rice","collard greens","Farofa or toasted breadcrumbs to serve (optional)"],"steps":["Soak the beans overnight in cold water, drain, and rinse.","Brown the pork, sausage and bacon in batches in a large pot, then set aside, keeping the fat in the pot.","Fry the onion and garlic in the same pot until soft, then add the cumin and oregano.","Return the meats to the pot, add the beans, bay leaves and enough water to cover by 3cm.","Bring to a boil, skim, then reduce to a very gentle simmer, partly covered, for 2 to 2 and a half hours.","Stir occasionally, topping up with hot water if needed, until the beans are creamy and the meats are tender.","Taste and season with salt and pepper. The meats add salt, so taste before adding more.","Serve in deep bowls with white rice, sauteed collard greens, farofa and orange slices on the side."],"tip":"Soak the beans overnight and simmer gently. Rushed beans stay hard, and a hard boil breaks them into mush instead of creamy beans.","notes":["Black beans are essential. They turn creamy and dark, and colour the whole stew. Kidney beans make a different dish.","A mix of meats gives feijoada its depth. Use what you can find, but include something smoked, for the classic flavour.","Bay leaves should be removed before serving. They flavour the stew, but they are not pleasant to eat.","Orange slices are not a garnish. Their acidity cuts the richness of the pork and beans, so serve them generously."],"variations":["With chicken: replace half the pork with chicken thighs, added for the last 45 minutes, for a lighter version.","Vegetarian feijoada: use mushrooms, smoked tofu and extra beans with vegetable stock, keeping the same seasoning.","Pressure cooker version: cook the soaked beans and meats at pressure for 35 minutes, then simmer uncovered for 15 minutes to thicken."],"serving":"Serves 8, as a long family lunch, with white rice, collard greens sauteed with garlic, farofa and orange slices in the middle of the table. Each person serves themselves beans and meat over rice, adds greens and farofa, and eats orange between bites to refresh the palate. Serve with a cold drink and keep the pot on the stove, because second helpings are expected.","storage":"Feijoada keeps in the fridge for up to 4 days, and the flavour improves overnight. Reheat gently until bubbling, adding a splash of water if it has thickened. It freezes very well for up to 3 months, in portions, thawed overnight in the fridge. Rice, greens and farofa should be made fresh, or stored separately for 2 days.","mistakes":["Skipping the overnight soak. The beans take far longer and cook unevenly.","Boiling hard. The beans break up and the stew turns mushy, so keep a gentle simmer.","Adding salt too early. Salt can toughen beans, so season well at the end, after tasting.","Serving without the accompaniments. Rice, greens, farofa and orange are part of the dish, not extras."],"faq":[["Can I use tinned black beans?","Yes, in a pinch. Add them for the last 30 minutes with the browned meats, and reduce the water, but the stew will be less rich."],["Is feijoada very fatty?","It is a rich stew, made with pork and sausage. Use leaner cuts and drain excess fat after browning if you prefer a lighter version."],["What is farofa?","Toasted cassava flour, often with onion and bacon, sprinkled over the beans and rice. Toasted breadcrumbs are a fair substitute."],["Can I make feijoada ahead?","Yes, and it is better the next day. Cook it fully, cool, refrigerate, and reheat gently."]]},"meatpie":{"intro":"The Aussie meat pie is an icon, handheld, golden and eaten at football games, bakeries and kitchen tables all over Australia. Inside a crisp shortcrust base and a flaky puff pastry lid is a rich beef filling, slow cooked with onion, tomato, Worcestershire sauce and a little Vegemite for depth, thick enough to hold its shape when the pie is cut, but juicy enough to need tomato sauce on top. Make the filling ahead and cool it fully, because warm filling melts the pastry, then bake until the lid is deep golden. Serve it hot, with tomato sauce, and eat it with your hands, the Australian way.","ing":["Beef chuck 700g (diced small)","Onion 1","garlic 2 cloves","Tomato paste 2 tbsp","beef stock 400ml","Worcestershire sauce 2 tbsp","Vegemite 1 tsp","Flour 2 tbsp","thyme 1 tsp","Shortcrust pastry for bases","puff pastry for lids","Egg 1 (beaten, for glazing)","Tomato sauce to serve","salt","pepper"],"steps":["Brown the beef in batches in a hot pot, then set aside. Brown the onion and garlic in the same pot.","Add the tomato paste and flour, cook for 1 minute, then pour in the stock, stirring to lift the browned flavour.","Return the beef, add Worcestershire, Vegemite and thyme, and simmer gently for 60 to 75 minutes until tender and thick.","Cool the filling completely, in the fridge if possible. Warm filling will melt the pastry and make it soggy.","Line greased pie tins or a muffin tin with shortcrust pastry, fill with the cold beef mixture, and brush the edges with egg.","Top with puff pastry lids, press the edges to seal, trim, and cut a small steam hole in each lid.","Brush with beaten egg, and bake at 200C for 25 to 30 minutes, until deep golden and crisp.","Rest for 5 minutes, lift out of the tins, and serve hot with tomato sauce."],"tip":"Cool the filling completely before filling the pastry. Even slightly warm filling softens the pastry and stops it crisping in the oven.","notes":["Beef chuck becomes tender and rich with slow cooking. Lean stewing beef dries out, and mince makes a different, softer pie.","Vegemite adds a savoury depth that is hard to place but unmistakably Australian. Use only a little, or it will overpower the filling.","Shortcrust for the base gives structure, puff pastry for the lid gives flake. Using puff pastry for both makes the base soggy.","Egg wash gives the golden shine. Brush it on just before baking, and do not let it pool in the steam hole."],"variations":["With mushrooms: add sliced mushrooms with the onion, for extra flavour and a lighter filling.","Chicken pie: use chicken thighs with the same sauce, cooked for 40 minutes, for a chicken version.","Cheese and bacon pie: add grated cheese and cooked bacon to the cooled filling before baking."],"serving":"Serves 6, as individual pies, hot from the oven, with tomato sauce on top and a simple salad or peas on the side. Meat pies are handheld food, so serve them on plates with a fork nearby, but encourage eating with hands while they are hot. They are perfect for lunch, for picnics, and for watching sport, with extra tomato sauce on the table.","storage":"Cooked pies keep in the fridge for up to 3 days, covered, and reheat in the oven at 180C until hot and crisp, about 15 minutes. They freeze well for up to 3 months, baked or unbaked, thawed overnight in the fridge. The filling on its own keeps for 3 days in the fridge and freezes for 3 months. Do not microwave pies if you want a crisp crust.","mistakes":["Filling the pastry while warm. The pastry melts and goes soggy, so cool the filling fully.","Underbaking the base. The bottom stays pale and soft, so bake until the lid is deep golden and the base is set.","Forgetting the steam hole. Steam builds up and bursts the lid, so cut a small hole in each pie.","Making the filling too thin. It runs out when cut, so simmer until thick enough to mound on a spoon."],"faq":[["Can I use shop bought pastry?","Yes. Good quality shortcrust and puff pastry work well, and save a lot of time. Keep them cold until you use them."],["Can I make one large pie instead?","Yes. Use a 20cm pie dish, line it with shortcrust, fill, top with puff pastry, and bake for 40 to 45 minutes."],["What is Vegemite for?","It adds savoury, yeasty depth to the filling. If you cannot find it, use a little extra Worcestershire sauce and a pinch of salt."],["Can I freeze meat pies?","Yes, baked or unbaked, for up to 3 months. Bake from frozen, adding 10 to 15 minutes, until hot through."]]},"haleem":{"intro":"Haleem is slow food in the truest sense, a dish of patience that rewards hours of cooking with a thick, silky porridge and the famous stringy texture called resta. Wheat, barley and four lentils soak overnight, then cook until completely soft, while beef cooks separately until it shreds into fine fibres. The two are combined and mashed together in the traditional ghoota, until the grains and the meat become one. Haleem traces its roots to the Arab dish harees, brought to Hyderabad in the Mughal era and transformed over centuries into the rich dish loved across Pakistan today. It is the dish of Ramadan nights, Muharram gatherings and big family weekends, served hot with fried onions, ginger, green chillies, lemon and chaat masala.","ing":["Whole wheat berries (gandum) 1 cup (soaked overnight)","Barley (jau) ½ cup (soaked overnight)","Chana daal ½ cup","masoor daal ¼ cup","moong daal ¼ cup","mash daal ¼ cup","Beef 1 kg","beef bones 250 g","Ghee or oil 1 cup","onions 2 large (thinly sliced)","Ginger paste 2 tbsp","garlic paste 2 tbsp","yoghurt 1 cup (whisked)","Red chilli powder 2 tbsp","Kashmiri chilli powder 1 tbsp","coriander powder 1½ tbsp","turmeric powder 1 tsp","Haleem masala 2 tbsp","salt 2 tbsp","Cumin seeds 1 tbsp","black peppercorns 1 tsp","cloves 6 to 8","green cardamom 5","black cardamom 2","cinnamon sticks 2","mace 2 blades","nutmeg ¼ piece","star anise 1","Ghee ½ cup","onion 1 medium (thinly sliced, for the tarka)","Ginger 2 tbsp (julienned)","fresh coriander ½ cup (chopped)","fresh mint ½ cup (chopped)","green chillies 4","lemons 3 (for garnish)","Chaat masala (for dusting)"],"steps":["Rinse the wheat, barley and all the lentils until the water runs clear, cover with plenty of fresh water and soak overnight, or for at least 8 hours. Soak the masoor, moong and mash lentils for at least 2 hours.","Drain the soaked grains and lentils, put them in a large heavy pot with 12 cups of water, 1 tsp turmeric and 1 tsp salt, and simmer covered on low heat for 2 to 2.5 hours, until everything is completely soft and mushy.","Roast the cumin seeds, black peppercorns, cloves, green and black cardamom, cinnamon, mace, nutmeg and star anise in a dry pan for 1 minute, let them cool, and grind them into a fine powder.","Heat 1 cup of ghee or oil in a large heavy pot, fry the sliced onions until deep golden brown, and lift out half for garnish. Add the ginger and garlic pastes and fry for 1 minute.","Add the beef and bones and sear on high heat for 5 to 7 minutes, then add the red chilli powder, Kashmiri chilli powder, coriander powder, turmeric, haleem masala, salt and half the ground spice powder, and stir for 2 minutes. Pour in the whisked yoghurt and cook until the oil separates, then add 6 cups of water.","Cover and cook until the meat is completely tender and falling apart, 45 minutes in a pressure cooker or 2.5 hours in a regular pot. Lift out the meat, discard the bare bones, and shred the beef into fine fibres by hand with a masher or two forks. Save the korma gravy.","Blend the cooked grains and lentils lightly with a hand blender, leaving some texture, and pour in the shredded beef with the saved gravy. Mash and stir vigorously on low heat for 30 to 45 minutes, adding the remaining spice powder, until the mixture turns thick, sticky and stringy.","Heat ½ cup of ghee in a small pan, fry the sliced onion until crisp and golden, and pour the hot ghee and onion over the haleem. Cover for 5 minutes, then serve hot in bowls with fried onions, ginger, coriander, mint, green chillies, lemon wedges and chaat masala, with warm naan on the side."],"tip":"Soak the wheat and barley for at least 12 hours, and mash the meat by hand. A food processor cuts the meat fibres too short and ruins the stringy resta texture. Use a heavy-bottomed pot and stir often, because thick haleem catches and burns easily.","notes":["Wheat berries and barley need a long soak, at least 8 hours and ideally overnight, or they stay gritty and take much longer to break down.","Beef from the leg or shoulder, in large chunks, gives the long fibres that make the stringy resta texture. Bones add body and richness to the pot.","Haleem masala varies in strength by brand. Use the stated amount the first time, and adjust next time.","Ghee gives the traditional flavour. Oil works too, and half ghee and half oil is a good middle way."],"variations":["Chicken haleem: replace the beef with the same weight of bone-in chicken, which cooks much faster and gives a lighter flavour.","Mutton haleem: use goat meat or lamb shoulder for a richer flavour, traditional in royal Mughlai kitchens.","Vegetarian oats haleem: skip the meat, double the lentils, add rolled oats, and use vegetable stock with extra fried onions for savoury depth."],"serving":"Serve hot in wide, shallow bowls, with the garnishes on a platter on the table so each person can add fried onions, ginger, coriander, mint, green chillies, lemon and chaat masala to taste. Haleem is traditionally served with hot tandoori naan, or eaten on its own with a spoon. Bring the pot to the table while it is still bubbling.","storage":"Cool and refrigerate in an airtight container for up to 3 days. The haleem thickens as it cools. Reheat gently on low to medium heat with a splash of water or meat broth, stirring constantly so it does not stick. Freeze for up to 3 months, thaw overnight in the fridge, and add the garnish fresh at serving only.","mistakes":["Using a food processor on the meat. It cuts the fibres short and gives a grainy soup instead of stringy haleem.","Skipping the overnight soak. The grains stay gritty and take far longer to break down.","Cooking on high heat and not stirring. Thick haleem catches on the bottom and burns.","Serving it too thin. Keep simmering and mashing on low heat until the starch thickens it into a porridge that mounds on the spoon."],"faq":[["Can I freeze Haleem?","Yes. Haleem freezes exceptionally well. Let it cool completely, divide it into airtight containers and freeze for up to 3 months. Thaw overnight in the fridge before reheating."],["How do I reheat Haleem?","Haleem thickens a lot as it cools. Put it in a pot with a splash of water or meat broth and heat on low to medium heat, stirring constantly so it does not stick to the bottom."],["Can I make Haleem gluten-free?","Traditional haleem relies on wheat and barley, which contain gluten. To make it gluten-free, replace the wheat and barley with rolled oats and brown rice."],["What should I do if my Haleem is too thin?","Keep simmering it uncovered on low heat while mashing with a wooden spoon. The starch from the lentils and wheat thickens the mixture as the extra moisture evaporates."]]}};
